/* Russian Alphabet & Reading — FREE Foundation (for English speakers).
   MIRRORED language fields: en/themeEn/say = RUSSIAN (target, Cyrillic + stress marks + ё);
   ru/themeRu/gloss = ENGLISH (native). Мира narrates in English (informal "you").
   Visible Russian carries an acute stress mark; TTS twins (introAudio, rules[].audio,
   examples[].say, data-say) use PLAIN Cyrillic (no combining accents). ё always written.
   Lesson 1 hand-authored as the exemplar; lessons 2–12 follow the same shape. */
const DAYS = {
"1": {"day":1,"week":"01","themeRu":"True friends","themeEn":"А К М О Т","intro":"Hi, I'm Мира — and I'll be right next to you the whole way. Today is the gentlest possible start: five Russian letters that look like English letters <b>and</b> sound like them. By the end of this lesson you'll read a real Russian word out loud. Not a fake practice one — a real one. Ready? Let's go slowly. 💛","introAudio":"Hi, I'm Mira, and I'll be right next to you the whole way. Today is the gentlest possible start: five Russian letters that look like English letters and sound like them. By the end of this lesson you'll read a real Russian word out loud. A real one. Ready? Let's go slowly.","goals":["Recognise five Russian letters: <b>А, К, М, О, Т</b>","Match each letter to its sound","Sound out and read your first real Russian word — <b>кот</b> (cat)","Read a handful of real words aloud with confidence"],"learned":["Recognised five Russian letters — <b>А, К, М, О, Т</b>","Matched each letter to its sound","Read your first real Russian word aloud — <b>кот</b> (cat)","Read several real Russian words on your own"],"review":null,"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Letters 1 · Old friends","title":"Five letters you <em>already</em> know","paras":["Cyrillic looks scary from far away — but some letters are old friends. These five look almost exactly like English letters, and they sound almost exactly the same too. No tricks here.","Tap the 🔊 on each one and just listen. Say it back softly. That's the whole job right now."],"audio":"Cyrillic looks scary from far away, but some letters are old friends. These five look almost exactly like English letters, and they sound almost the same too. Tap the speaker on each one and just listen. Say it back softly.","table":{"rows":[["А <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"а\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>a</b> in “father”"],["К <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ка\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>k</b> in “kite”"],["М <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ма\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>m</b> in “moon”"],["О <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"о\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>o</b> in “more” (when stressed)"],["Т <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тэ\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>t</b> in “top”"]]},"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. Wrong taps are totally fine — that's how it sticks.","q":"Which sound does the letter <b>М</b> make?","opts":["m (as in “moon”)","n (as in “noon”)","w (as in “win”)"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>М</b> is just like English <b>m</b>. Same shape, same sound — an old friend."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which sound does the letter <b>Т</b> make?","opts":["d","t","g"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Т</b> = <b>t</b>. It even looks like a capital T — same shape, same sound."}]},{"eyebrow":"Reading 1 · Your first word","title":"Sound out <em>your first Russian word</em>","paras":["Now put three of them together: <b>К · О · Т</b>. Say each sound slowly — “k… o… t” — then faster: <b>ко́т</b>. That's the Russian word for <b>cat</b>.","The little mark over the <b>о</b> (о́) just tells you which part to stress — say that part a bit louder. Russians don't write this mark in real life; we add it only to help you while you learn."],"audio":"Now put three of them together: К, О, Т. Say each sound slowly: k, o, t. Then faster: кот. That's the Russian word for cat. The little mark over the о just tells you which part to say louder. We add it only to help you while you learn.","examples":[{"ru":"cat","en":"<b>ко́т</b>","gloss":"[kot]","say":"кот"},{"ru":"mama, mum","en":"<b>ма́ма</b>","gloss":"[MA-ma]","say":"мама"}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the letters in the right order to spell the word.","q":"Spell “cat” in Russian (say the sounds as you tap: k-o-t):","answer":"к о т"},{"type":"choice","q":"You see <b>ко́т</b>. What does it mean?","opts":["dog","cat","cow"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ко́т</b> = <b>cat</b>. You just read your first Russian word. That's real reading. 🐱"}]},{"eyebrow":"Reading 2 · A few more","title":"More real words — same five letters","paras":["You can already read several words with just these five letters. Sound each one out, then check the meaning. Remember: the о́ mark shows the loud part.","Notice how <b>ма́ма</b> is just <b>м-а-м-а</b> — exactly what your eye expects. Russian is far more “what-you-see-is-what-you-say” than English."],"audio":"You can already read several words with just these five letters. Sound each one out, then check the meaning. Notice how мама is just m-a-m-a, exactly what your eye expects.","examples":[{"ru":"poppy (flower)","en":"<b>ма́к</b>","gloss":"[mak]","say":"мак"},{"ru":"there / over there","en":"<b>та́м</b>","gloss":"[tam]","say":"там"},{"ru":"atom","en":"<b>а́том</b>","gloss":"[A-tom]","say":"атом"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Read it aloud: <b>ма́ма</b>. What does it mean?","opts":["mum / mama","map","meat"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>ма́ма</b> = <b>mum</b>. It even sounds like it — a true friend across languages."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the meaning in English.","q":"You read <b>та́м</b>. Write what it means in English:","accept":["there","over there"],"placeholder":"in English…"},{"type":"speak","target":"а́том","sub":"[A-tom] — say the first part a little louder","want":"атом"}]}],"vocab":{"intro":"Your first Russian words. Flip a card to see the sound and the meaning. Every word here uses only today's five letters.","items":[{"en":"ко́т","ru":"cat","ex":"[kot] — a cat"},{"en":"ма́ма","ru":"mum, mama","ex":"[MA-ma] — mother"},{"en":"ма́к","ru":"poppy","ex":"[mak] — the flower"},{"en":"та́м","ru":"there","ex":"[tam] — over there"},{"en":"а́том","ru":"atom","ex":"[A-tom] — an atom"},{"en":"ка́к","ru":"how","ex":"[kak] — how / like"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Quick memory game. Drag each Russian letter to the sound it makes.","pairs":[["А","a (father)"],["К","k (kite)"],["М","m (moon)"],["О","o (more)"],["Т","t (top)"]]},"reading":{"intro":"Read these real words aloud, slowly. Tap 🔊 to hear them. You can read every one of these now.","title":"Чита́ем! (Let's read)","sentences":["ко́т","ма́ма","ма́к","та́м","ка́к","а́том"],"translation":"cat · mum · poppy · there · how · atom"},"quiz":{"intro":"Last step — a few quick checks, then your very first lesson is done. I believe in you. 💛","items":[{"q":"1. The letter <b>Р</b> is coming next lesson — but for now, which sound does <b>Т</b> make?","opts":["t","p","r"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Т</b> = <b>t</b>. (Careful — some Russian letters only <i>look</i> like English ones. That's tomorrow's adventure.)"},{"q":"2. Read it: <b>ко́т</b> means —","opts":["cat","dog","milk"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>ко́т</b> = <b>cat</b>. Your first word — you own it now."},{"q":"3. Which word means “mum”?","opts":["ма́к","ма́ма","та́м"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ма́ма</b> = mum. <b>ма́к</b> = poppy, <b>та́м</b> = there."},{"q":"4. The little mark in <b>ко́т</b> (о́) tells you —","opts":["to whisper that part","which part to say louder (stress)","to skip that letter"],"answer":1,"explain":"It marks the <b>stress</b> — the part you say a little louder. Native text hides it; we show it to help you."},{"q":"5. Which of these letters did we meet today?","opts":["А, К, М, О, Т","B, D, F, G, J","Ж, Ц, Щ, Ы, Ъ"],"answer":0,"explain":"Today's five old friends: <b>А, К, М, О, Т</b>. The scary-looking ones (Ж, Ц, Щ…) come much later — and they'll be friends too."}]}},
"2": {"day":2,"week":"01","themeRu":"False friends","themeEn":"В Е Н Р С У Х","intro":"Welcome back — lesson two, and it's the famous one. Yesterday's five letters played fair. Today's seven wear English costumes: they look exactly like B, E, H, P, C, Y and X, but every single one hides a different sound. The one that looks like P is really a rolled r; the C-shape says s; the H-shape says n. Trust your ears, not your eyes, and by the end you'll read the Russian words for nose, mouth and no. Let's unmask them together. 💛","introAudio":"Welcome back, lesson two, and it's the famous one. Yesterday's five letters played fair. Today's seven wear English costumes: they look exactly like B, E, H, P, C, Y and X, but every single one hides a different sound. The one that looks like P is really a rolled r. The C-shape says s. The H-shape says n. Trust your ears, not your eyes, and by the end you'll read the Russian words for nose, mouth and no. Let's unmask them together.","goals":["Meet the seven “false friends” — letters that look like English <b>B, E, H, P, C, Y, X</b> but sound different","Un-learn the trap: the P-shape says r, the C-shape says s, the H-shape says n","Match each new letter to its real sound: v, ye, n, r, s, oo, kh","Read seven real Russian words aloud, including “nose”, “mouth” and the famous word for “no”"],"learned":["Met the seven “false friends” — letters that look like English <b>B, E, H, P, C, Y, X</b> but sound different","Un-learned the trap: the P-shape says r, the C-shape says s, the H-shape says n","Matched each new letter to its real sound: v, ye, n, r, s, oo, kh","Read seven real Russian words aloud, including “nose”, “mouth” and the famous word for “no”"],"review":{"intro":"Before the new letters, a thirty-second look back at yesterday's five true friends. They'll appear inside every word today, so let's wake them up.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. Wrong taps are part of the learning.","q":"Which sound does <b>О</b> make when it's stressed?","opts":["e (as in “men”)","o (as in “more”)","i (as in “sit”)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Stressed <b>О</b> = <b>o</b> as in “more”. Your five true friends are still true."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the letters in the right order to spell the word.","q":"Spell “mum” in Russian (say the sounds as you tap: m-a-m-a):","answer":"м а м а"},{"type":"choice","q":"Read it: <b>ко́т</b> means —","opts":["coat","kite","cat"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>ко́т</b> = <b>cat</b> — your very first word from yesterday. Still yours."}],"introAudio":"Before the new letters, a thirty-second look back at yesterday's five true friends. They'll appear inside every word today, so let's wake them up."},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Letters 2 · False friends","title":"Seven letters that <em>lie</em> to your eyes","paras":["Here's the trap that catches every English reader: these seven letters look like old friends, but their sounds moved house. <b>В</b> looks like B yet says [v]. <b>Р</b> looks like P yet says a rolled [r]. <b>С</b> looks like C yet always says [s]. <b>Н</b> looks like H yet says [n].","The friendlier three: <b>Е</b> says [ye] as in “yes”, <b>У</b> says [oo] as in “moon”, and <b>Х</b> is a breathy [kh], like the Scottish “loch”. New sounds, but nothing your mouth can't do.","Don't try to memorise them all at once. Tap each 🔊, listen, and let the surprise do the work — that little jolt of “wait, that's not p?” is exactly what makes it stick."],"audio":"Here's the trap that catches every English reader: these seven letters look like old friends, but their sounds moved house. В looks like B yet says v. Р looks like P yet says a rolled r. С looks like C yet always says s. Н looks like H yet says n. The friendlier three: Е says ye as in yes, У says oo as in moon, and Х is a breathy kh, like the Scottish loch. Don't try to memorise them all at once. Tap each speaker, listen, and let the surprise do the work.","table":{"rows":[["В <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вэ\">🔊</button>","looks like <b>B</b> — but sounds like <b>v</b> in “van”"],["Е <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"е\">🔊</button>","looks like <b>E</b> — but sounds like <b>ye</b> in “yes”"],["Н <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"на\">🔊</button>","looks like <b>H</b> — but sounds like <b>n</b> in “noon”"],["Р <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ра\">🔊</button>","looks like <b>P</b> — but sounds like a rolled <b>r</b>"],["С <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"са\">🔊</button>","looks like <b>C</b> — but always sounds like <b>s</b> in “sun”"],["У <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"у\">🔊</button>","looks like <b>Y</b> — but sounds like <b>oo</b> in “moon”"],["Х <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ха\">🔊</button>","looks like <b>X</b> — but sounds like <b>kh</b> in Scottish “loch”"]]},"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the sound the letter really makes — not the one your eyes expect.","q":"The letter <b>В</b> looks like English B. Which sound does it really make?","opts":["v (as in “van”)","b (as in “bat”)","w (as in “win”)"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>В</b> = <b>v</b>. The b sound has its own Russian letter (Б) — you'll meet it next lesson."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which sound does <b>Р</b> make?","opts":["p (as in “pot”)","d (as in “dog”)","r (rolled, like a Spanish r)"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>Р</b> is a rolled <b>r</b> — never p. This is the single most famous trap in the alphabet, and you just walked past it."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which sound does <b>С</b> make — always, in every word?","opts":["k (as in “cat”)","s (as in “sun”)","ts (as in “cats”)"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>С</b> = <b>s</b>, no exceptions — Russian С never switches to a k sound the way English c does. The k sound is <b>К</b>, which you already know."}]},{"eyebrow":"Reading 1 · New sounds, real words","title":"Sound out <em>но́с</em> — trust the new sounds","paras":["Time to read with the unmasked letters. Take <b>Н · О · С</b>: say n… o… s slowly, then faster — <b>но́с</b>. That's “nose”. Your eyes wanted to read “hoc”, but you read [nos] — the whole skill of this lesson, already working.","Two more from the same mould: <b>ро́т</b> [rot] is “mouth” — roll that r a little — and <b>со́н</b> [son] is “sleep” or “a dream”. And со́н is но́с flipped — the same three letters in a different order."],"audio":"Time to read with the unmasked letters. Take Н, О, С: say n, o, s slowly, then faster: нос. That's nose. Your eyes wanted to read something else, but you read nos. Two more from the same mould: рот is mouth, roll that r a little, and сон is sleep or a dream. Same three letters as нос, in a different order.","examples":[{"ru":"nose","en":"<b>но́с</b>","gloss":"[nos]","say":"нос"},{"ru":"mouth","en":"<b>ро́т</b>","gloss":"[rot]","say":"рот"},{"ru":"sleep, a dream","en":"<b>со́н</b>","gloss":"[son]","say":"сон"}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the letters in the right order to spell the word.","q":"Spell “nose” in Russian (say the sounds as you tap: n-o-s):","answer":"н о с"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type how the word sounds, in English letters.","q":"How does <b>ро́т</b> (mouth) sound? Write it in English letters:","accept":["rot"],"placeholder":"in English letters…"},{"type":"choice","q":"Read it: <b>со́н</b>. What does it mean?","opts":["nose","juice","sleep / a dream"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>со́н</b> = sleep or a dream. The nose is <b>но́с</b> — same letters, flipped. Russian loves this little pair."}]},{"eyebrow":"Reading 2 · Е, У and Х at work","title":"The word you already know — <em>не́т</em>","paras":["You've heard this word in a hundred films: <b>не́т</b> [nyet] — “no”. See the <b>Е</b> doing its job? It isn't English e; it says [ye], so н-е-т comes out as “nyet”, not “net”.","Now the breathy <b>Х</b>. It's the sound at the end of Scottish “loch” — a soft rasp at the back of the mouth, not a hard k. Put it after <b>у</b> [oo] and you can read <b>му́ха</b> [MOO-kha], “a fly”, and <b>уха́</b> [oo-KHA], a much-loved Russian fish soup.","One more gift: the name <b>Ве́ра</b> [VYE-ra] — Vera, which also means “faith”. Four letters, three of them today's false friends, and you can read every single one."],"audio":"You've heard this word in a hundred films: нет. No. See the Е doing its job? It isn't English e; it says ye, so the word comes out as nyet, not net. Now the breathy Х. It's the sound at the end of Scottish loch, a soft rasp at the back of the mouth. Put it after у and you can read муха, a fly, and уха, a much-loved Russian fish soup. One more gift: the name Вера, which also means faith. Four letters, and you can read every single one.","examples":[{"ru":"no","en":"<b>не́т</b>","gloss":"[nyet]","say":"нет"},{"ru":"fly (the insect)","en":"<b>му́ха</b>","gloss":"[MOO-kha]","say":"муха"},{"ru":"fish soup","en":"<b>уха́</b>","gloss":"[oo-KHA]","say":"уха"},{"ru":"Vera (a name; also “faith”)","en":"<b>Ве́ра</b>","gloss":"[VYE-ra]","say":"Вера"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Read it aloud: <b>уха́</b>. What is it?","opts":["fish soup","an ear","a sigh"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>уха́</b> [oo-KHA] = fish soup, stress on the end. (The ear is <b>у́хо</b> — a different word for another day.)"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the meaning in English.","q":"You read <b>не́т</b>. Write what it means in English:","accept":["no"],"placeholder":"in English…"},{"type":"speak","target":"му́ха","sub":"[MOO-kha] — say the first part louder; kh is the “loch” sound","want":"муха"}]}],"vocab":{"intro":"Seven real words, every one built from today's false friends plus yesterday's letters. Flip each card, say the word aloud, and enjoy how un-English it sounds.","items":[{"en":"но́с","ru":"nose","ex":"[nos] — your nose"},{"en":"ро́т","ru":"mouth","ex":"[rot] — your mouth"},{"en":"со́н","ru":"sleep, a dream","ex":"[son] — the same letters as “nose”, flipped"},{"en":"не́т","ru":"no","ex":"[nyet] — the opposite of yes"},{"en":"му́ха","ru":"fly (insect)","ex":"[MOO-kha] — the buzzing insect"},{"en":"уха́","ru":"fish soup","ex":"[oo-KHA] — a classic Russian soup"},{"en":"Ве́ра","ru":"Vera (a name; also “faith”)","ex":"[VYE-ra] — a common Russian name"}]},"drag":{"intro":"The trap-buster game. Drag each false friend to the sound it really makes — not the sound your eyes expect.","pairs":[["В","v (van)"],["Е","ye (yes)"],["Н","n (noon)"],["Р","r (rolled r)"],["С","s (sun)"],["У","oo (moon)"],["Х","kh (loch)"]]},"reading":{"intro":"Read each word aloud, slowly, trusting the new sounds. Tap 🔊 to check yourself. Every letter here is one you now know.","title":"Чита́ем! (Let's read)","sentences":["но́с","ро́т","со́н","не́т","уха́","му́ха","Ве́ра"],"translation":"nose · mouth · sleep · no · fish soup · fly · Vera"},"quiz":{"intro":"Last step. If you can dodge these seven traps, the trickiest part of the whole alphabet is already behind you. 💛","items":[{"q":"1. The letter <b>Р</b> makes which sound?","opts":["p","r (rolled)","f"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Р</b> = a rolled <b>r</b>, never p. English p gets its own Russian letter (П) next lesson."},{"q":"2. <b>С</b> sounds like —","opts":["c in “cat”","ch in “chat”","s in “sun”"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>С</b> is always <b>s</b>. The k sound is <b>К</b>, and ch has its own letter (Ч) coming later."},{"q":"3. <b>Н</b> looks like English H. What does it actually say?","opts":["h","n","m"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Н</b> = <b>n</b>. Russian has no exact English h — the closest sound is the breathy <b>Х</b> [kh]."},{"q":"4. Read it: <b>но́с</b> means —","opts":["nose","no","dream"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>но́с</b> = <b>nose</b>. “No” is <b>не́т</b>, and a dream is <b>со́н</b> — the letters of но́с, flipped."},{"q":"5. Which word means “no”?","opts":["но́с","уха́","не́т"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>не́т</b> [nyet] = no. <b>но́с</b> is your nose, and <b>уха́</b> is fish soup."}]}},
"3": {"day":3,"week":"01","themeRu":"New shapes · I","themeEn":"Б Г Д З Л П","intro":"Welcome back! Last time the letters looked familiar but sounded strange. Today it's the opposite — six brand-new shapes hiding six sounds you've known your whole life: b, g, d, z, l, p. Once your eye learns the shapes, the sounds come free — and suddenly you can read <b>house</b>, <b>brother</b>, <b>friend</b> and <b>dad</b> in Russian. Let's meet them. 💛","introAudio":"Welcome back! Last time the letters looked familiar but sounded strange. Today it's the opposite: six brand-new shapes hiding six sounds you've known your whole life: b, g, d, z, l, p. Once your eye learns the shapes, the sounds come free, and suddenly you can read house, brother, friend and dad in Russian. Let's meet them.","goals":["Recognise six new letters: <b>Б, Г, Д, З, Л, П</b> — new shapes, familiar sounds","Match each new shape to a sound your mouth already knows","Build and read real words — <b>до́м</b> (house) and <b>бра́т</b> (brother)","Read seven real Russian words aloud, including <b>го́род</b> (city)"],"learned":["Recognised six new letters — <b>Б, Г, Д, З, Л, П</b>","Matched each new shape to a sound you already knew","Built and read <b>до́м</b> (house) and <b>бра́т</b> (brother) from letter tokens","Read seven real words aloud — including <b>дру́г</b> (friend) and <b>го́род</b> (city)"],"review":{"intro":"Before anything new — a quick look back. Last lesson you met the tricksters: В, Е, Н, Р, С, У, Х. They look English but sound Russian. Let's make sure they can't fool you anymore.","introAudio":"Before anything new, a quick look back. Last lesson you met the tricksters. They look English but sound Russian. Let's make sure they can't fool you anymore.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. Wrong taps are working moments, not mistakes.","q":"Which sound does the Russian letter <b>С</b> make?","opts":["k (as in “kite”)","s (as in “sun”)","ch (as in “chat”)"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>С</b> looks like English C but always sounds like <b>s</b>. A classic false friend — and you caught it."},{"type":"choice","q":"Read it: <b>но́с</b> means —","opts":["no","night","nose"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>но́с</b> = <b>nose</b>: Н is [n], о́ is the stressed [o], С is [s]. (“No” is <b>не́т</b> — that one's next.)"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the meaning in English.","q":"You read <b>не́т</b>. Write what it means in English:","accept":["no"],"placeholder":"in English…"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Letters 3 · New shapes","title":"Six new shapes, six <em>familiar</em> sounds","paras":["These six letters are the friendliest kind of new: the shapes are unfamiliar, but every sound is one your mouth already makes in English — b, g, d, z, l, p. Nothing to un-learn this time, only new outfits for old sounds.","Two tiny watch-outs. <b>Б</b> is the real [b] — don't hand that job to <b>В</b>, which you now know is [v]. And <b>З</b> looks like the number 3, but it's the buzzing [z] of “zoo”.","Tap the 🔊 on each letter, listen, and say it back softly. Notice how <b>Д</b> looks a bit like a little house on legs — it will spell one in a minute."],"audio":"These six letters are the friendliest kind of new: the shapes are unfamiliar, but every sound is one your mouth already makes in English: b, g, d, z, l, p. Two tiny watch-outs. Б is the real b sound. Don't hand that job to В, which you now know is v. And З looks like the number three, but it's the buzzing z of zoo. Tap the speaker on each letter, listen, and say it back softly.","table":{"rows":[["Б <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"бэ\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>b</b> in “book”"],["Г <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"гэ\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>g</b> in “go”"],["Д <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дэ\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>d</b> in “dog”"],["З <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"зэ\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>z</b> in “zoo”"],["Л <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ла\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>l</b> in “lamp”"],["П <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пэ\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>p</b> in “park”"]]},"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which sound does <b>Б</b> make?","opts":["b (as in “book”)","v (as in “vote”)","d (as in “dog”)"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Б</b> = <b>b</b>. The [v] sound belongs to <b>В</b>, the false friend from last lesson. The little flag on top says “b”."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which sound does <b>З</b> make?","opts":["s (as in “sun”)","v (as in “vote”)","z (as in “zoo”)"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>З</b> looks like the number 3, but it's the buzzing <b>z</b>. Its quiet cousin [s] is written <b>С</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which sound does <b>П</b> make?","opts":["n (as in “noon”)","p (as in “park”)","t (as in “top”)"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>П</b> = <b>p</b> — like the Greek letter pi. The [n] sound is written <b>Н</b>, remember?"}]},{"eyebrow":"Reading 5 · A house and a yes","title":"Sound out <em>дом</em> — and say “yes” in Russian","paras":["Put three letters to work: <b>Д · О · М</b>. Say each sound slowly — “d… o… m” — then faster: <b>до́м</b>. That's the Russian word for <b>house</b> (and <b>home</b>). A whole house in three letters.","And here's the shortest word of the day: <b>да́</b> — “yes”. Two letters, one nod. Say it out loud: [da]. Then meet <b>бра́т</b> — “brother” — four sounds in a row, exactly as written."],"audio":"Put three letters to work: Д, О, М. Say each sound slowly: d, o, m. Then faster: дом. That's the Russian word for house. And here's the shortest word of the day: да. Yes. Two letters, one nod. Then meet брат. Brother. Four sounds in a row, exactly as written.","examples":[{"ru":"house, home","en":"<b>до́м</b>","gloss":"[dom]","say":"дом"},{"ru":"yes","en":"<b>да́</b>","gloss":"[da]","say":"да"},{"ru":"brother","en":"<b>бра́т</b>","gloss":"[brat]","say":"брат"}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the letters in the right order to spell the word.","q":"Spell “house” in Russian (say the sounds as you tap: d-o-m):","answer":"д о м"},{"type":"build","q":"Now spell “brother” (b-r-a-t):","answer":"б р а т"},{"type":"choice","q":"You see <b>да́</b>. What does it mean?","opts":["no","dad","yes"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>да́</b> = <b>yes</b>. “No” is <b>не́т</b>, and “dad” — <b>па́па</b> — is waiting just below."}]},{"eyebrow":"Reading 6 · Four more words","title":"A friend, a table, a dad, <em>a city</em>","paras":["Four more real words, all from letters you own. One of them sets a small trap: <b>сто́л</b> looks like English “stool”, but it means <b>table</b>. The eye guesses; the reader checks.","<b>го́род</b> (city) is the longest word today — the mark shows the stress lands on the first part: GO-rod. Take it slowly, sound by sound, and the word will simply appear.","One more thing: these little pronunciation guides say each word exactly as it’s spelled. Real Russian speech relaxes a few sounds here and there — the rules lessons at the end of this course teach you exactly those small shifts."],"audio":"Four more real words, all from letters you own. One of them sets a small trap: стол looks like English stool, but it means table. The eye guesses; the reader checks. Город, city, is the longest word today. The stress lands on the first part. Take it slowly, sound by sound, and the word will simply appear. One more thing: these pronunciation guides say each word exactly as it is spelled. Real Russian speech relaxes a few sounds here and there. The rules lessons at the end of this course teach you exactly those small shifts.","examples":[{"ru":"friend","en":"<b>дру́г</b>","gloss":"[droog]","say":"друг"},{"ru":"table","en":"<b>сто́л</b>","gloss":"[stol]","say":"стол"},{"ru":"dad","en":"<b>па́па</b>","gloss":"[PA-pa]","say":"папа"},{"ru":"city","en":"<b>го́род</b>","gloss":"[GO-rod]","say":"город"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Read it aloud: <b>сто́л</b>. What does it mean?","opts":["table","stool","chair"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>сто́л</b> = <b>table</b>. It sounds like “stool”, but the meaning moved up a level — a favourite trick question among Russian teachers."},{"type":"type","q":"You read <b>дру́г</b>. Write what it means in English:","accept":["friend","a friend"],"placeholder":"in English…"},{"type":"speak","target":"го́род","sub":"[GO-rod] — say the first part a little louder","want":"город"}]}],"vocab":{"intro":"Seven real words, every one built from letters you now own. Flip a card to see the sound and the meaning.","items":[{"en":"до́м","ru":"house, home","ex":"[dom] — a house"},{"en":"да́","ru":"yes","ex":"[da] — yes"},{"en":"бра́т","ru":"brother","ex":"[brat] — a brother"},{"en":"дру́г","ru":"friend","ex":"[droog] — a friend"},{"en":"сто́л","ru":"table","ex":"[stol] — a table"},{"en":"па́па","ru":"dad","ex":"[PA-pa] — dad"},{"en":"го́род","ru":"city","ex":"[GO-rod] — a city"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Quick memory game. Drag each new letter to the sound it makes.","pairs":[["Б","b (book)"],["Г","g (go)"],["Д","d (dog)"],["З","z (zoo)"],["Л","l (lamp)"],["П","p (park)"]]},"reading":{"intro":"Read these real words aloud, slowly. Tap 🔊 to hear them. Every single one is yours now.","title":"Чита́ем! (Let's read)","sentences":["до́м","да́","бра́т","дру́г","сто́л","па́па","го́род"],"translation":"house · yes · brother · friend · table · dad · city"},"quiz":{"intro":"Last step — five quick checks, and lesson three is yours. Take your time. 💛","items":[{"q":"1. Which sound does <b>Б</b> make?","opts":["v","b","d"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Б</b> = <b>b</b>. Careful with its neighbour <b>В</b> — that false friend from last lesson is [v]."},{"q":"2. Read it: <b>до́м</b> means —","opts":["cat","table","house"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>до́м</b> = <b>house</b> (or home). <b>ко́т</b> is the cat, <b>сто́л</b> is the table."},{"q":"3. Which word means “brother”?","opts":["дру́г","бра́т","па́па"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>бра́т</b> = brother. <b>дру́г</b> = friend, <b>па́па</b> = dad."},{"q":"4. Which sound does <b>Л</b> make?","opts":["l (as in “lamp”)","p (as in “park”)","n (as in “noon”)"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Л</b> = <b>l</b>. It looks a little like <b>П</b> [p], but the left leg of Л bends — a lamp with a kink."},{"q":"5. Which word means “city”?","opts":["сто́л","да́","го́род"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>го́род</b> = city — GO-rod, stress on the first part. <b>сто́л</b> = table, <b>да́</b> = yes. You read all of these today."}]}},
"4": {"day":4,"week":"01","themeRu":"New shapes · II","themeEn":"И Й Ф Э","intro":"Welcome back — it's Мира. Today we finish the “new shape, familiar sound” family: four more letters that look unusual but hide sounds your mouth already knows. One of them is a whole word all by itself, and another comes with a tiny hat. By the end of this lesson you'll read your first complete Russian sentence — a real one, start to finish. Let's take it slowly, as always. 💛","introAudio":"Welcome back, it's Mira. Today we finish the new shape, familiar sound family: four more letters that look unusual but hide sounds your mouth already knows. One of them is a whole word all by itself, and another comes with a tiny hat. By the end of this lesson you'll read your first complete Russian sentence, a real one, start to finish. Let's take it slowly, as always.","goals":["Recognise four new letters: <b>И, Й, Ф, Э</b>","Hear the difference between <b>И</b> (a full vowel) and <b>Й</b> (a short glide)","Read your first complete Russian sentence — <b>Э́то до́м.</b> (This is a house.)","Read real words like <b>зима́</b> (winter), <b>музе́й</b> (museum) and <b>трамва́й</b> (tram) aloud"],"learned":["Recognised four new letters — <b>И, Й, Ф, Э</b>","Heard and used the difference between <b>И</b> and <b>Й</b>","Read your first complete Russian sentence — <b>Э́то до́м.</b>","Read real words like <b>зима́</b>, <b>музе́й</b> and <b>трамва́й</b> aloud"],"review":{"intro":"First, a quick look back at yesterday's six letters — Б, Г, Д, З, Л, П. A minute of remembering now makes today's letters stick better.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. Wrong taps are working moments — they help it stick.","q":"Which sound does the letter <b>Б</b> make?","opts":["v (as in “van”)","b (as in “book”)","d (as in “dog”)"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Б</b> = <b>b</b>. The v-sound belongs to <b>В</b> — the false friend from Lesson 2."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the letters in the right order to spell the word.","q":"Spell “brother” in Russian (sound it out: b-r-a-t):","answer":"б р а т"},{"type":"choice","q":"Read it: <b>до́м</b> means —","opts":["house","city","table"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>до́м</b> = <b>house</b>. You read it yesterday — and it's still yours today. (<b>го́род</b> = city, <b>сто́л</b> = table.)"}],"introAudio":"First, a quick look back at yesterday's six letters — Б, Г, Д, З, Л, П. A minute of remembering now makes today's letters stick better."},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Letters 4 · New shapes II","title":"И — and its short twin <em>Й</em>","paras":["Meet <b>И</b>: it looks like a backwards N, but it's a vowel — it sounds like <b>ee</b> in “see”. And here's a lovely bonus: <b>и</b> on its own is a whole Russian word. It means <b>and</b>. One letter, one word — you can already read it.","Now look at <b>Й</b> — the same letter wearing a tiny hat. Russians call it <b>и кра́ткое</b>, “short И”, and the name is honest: it's not a full vowel, just a quick <b>y</b> glide, like the y at the end of “boy”. Compare: <b>И</b> you can stretch — “eeee” — but <b>Й</b> is over in an instant. You'll meet it at the end of words like <b>трамва́й</b> (tram) and <b>музе́й</b> (museum)."],"audio":"Meet И: it looks like a backwards N, but it's a vowel. It sounds like ee in see. And here's a lovely bonus: и on its own is a whole Russian word. It means and. Now look at Й, the same letter wearing a tiny hat. Russians call it и краткое, short И, and the name is honest: it's not a full vowel, just a quick y glide, like the y at the end of boy. И you can stretch, but Й is over in an instant.","table":{"rows":[["И <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"и\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>ee</b> in “see” — a full vowel you can stretch"],["Й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ай\">🔊</button>","a quick <b>y</b> glide, as in “bo<b>y</b>” — never a full vowel"]]},"examples":[{"ru":"and","en":"<b>и</b>","gloss":"[ee]","say":"и"},{"ru":"winter","en":"<b>зима́</b>","gloss":"[zee-MA]","say":"зима"},{"ru":"world; peace","en":"<b>ми́р</b>","gloss":"[meer]","say":"мир"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which of these is a <b>full vowel</b> you can stretch out?","opts":["Й (with the hat)","И (no hat)","both are full vowels"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>И</b> is the full vowel — “eeee”. <b>Й</b> is its short twin: a quick glide, gone in an instant."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the letters in the right order to spell the word.","q":"Spell “winter” in Russian (sound it out: z-ee-m-a):","answer":"з и м а"},{"type":"choice","q":"The word <b>трамва́й</b> ends in <b>Й</b>. How does that ending sound?","opts":["a stretched “ee” — tram-va-ee","a quick “y” glide — tram-VAY, like “vie”","the Й is silent"],"answer":1,"explain":"That's the whole job of <b>Й</b>: a short glide that closes the syllable — <b>трамва́й</b> [tram-VAY]. No stretching, no silence."}]},{"eyebrow":"Letters 5 · Two more","title":"Ф says <em>f</em> — and Э is the honest <em>e</em>","paras":["<b>Ф</b> looks like a circle on a stick, but the sound is plain old <b>f</b>, as in “fun”. Nothing hides behind it: <b>фла́г</b> is exactly what it looks like once you know the letters — <b>flag</b>.","<b>Э</b> is a backwards E, and it sounds like <b>e</b> in “end”. Careful with one thing: back in Lesson 2 you met <b>Е</b>, which says <b>ye</b> (as in “yes”). <b>Э</b> is the plain one — no y in front. That's why <b>э́то</b> (this) starts with a clean “e”, and why it's such a useful word: point at anything and say <b>э́то</b>."],"audio":"Ф looks like a circle on a stick, but the sound is plain old f, as in fun. Флаг is exactly what it looks like once you know the letters: flag. Э is a backwards E, and it sounds like e in end. Careful with one thing: in Lesson 2 you met Е, which says ye, as in yes. Э is the plain one, no y in front. That's why это, this, starts with a clean e.","table":{"rows":[["Ф <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"фа\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>f</b> in “fun”"],["Э <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"э\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>e</b> in “end” — plain, no “y” in front (that one is Е)"]]},"examples":[{"ru":"flag","en":"<b>фла́г</b>","gloss":"[flag]","say":"флаг"},{"ru":"echo","en":"<b>э́хо</b>","gloss":"[E-kho]","say":"эхо"},{"ru":"this / this is","en":"<b>э́то</b>","gloss":"[E-to]","say":"это"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which sound does <b>Ф</b> make?","opts":["p (as in “pen”)","v (as in “van”)","f (as in “fun”)"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>Ф</b> = <b>f</b>. The v-sound is <b>В</b>, and p is <b>П</b> — you know both already."},{"type":"build","q":"Spell “this” in Russian (sound it out: e-t-o):","answer":"э т о"},{"type":"choice","q":"<b>Э</b> and <b>Е</b> look related. What's the real difference?","opts":["Э = plain “e” (end); Е = “ye” (yes)","they sound exactly the same","Э = “ye” (yes); Е = plain “e” (end)"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Э</b> is the plain “e” of “end”; <b>Е</b> carries a hidden y — “ye” as in “yes”. Swapping them is the classic mix-up, and now it can't catch you."}]},{"eyebrow":"Reading 3 · A real sentence","title":"Your first <em>whole sentence</em>","paras":["Here's the moment: <b>э́то</b> means “this / this is”, so <b>Э́то до́м</b> is a complete Russian sentence — “This is a house.” Subject, meaning, full stop. You just crossed from reading words to reading Russian.","Now stretch it: <b>Э́то музе́й</b> — “This is a museum.” Sound out <b>музе́й</b> slowly — m-oo-z-ye… and close it with that quick <b>Й</b> glide. Take your time with <b>трамва́й</b> too; the ending is the same little glide."],"audio":"Here's the moment: это means this, or this is. So Это дом is a complete Russian sentence: this is a house. You just crossed from reading words to reading Russian. Now stretch it: Это музей, this is a museum. Sound out музей slowly and close it with that quick Й glide. Take your time with трамвай too; the ending is the same little glide.","examples":[{"ru":"This is a house.","en":"<b>Э́то до́м.</b>","gloss":"[E-to dom]","say":"это дом"},{"ru":"museum","en":"<b>музе́й</b>","gloss":"[moo-ZYEY]","say":"музей"},{"ru":"tram","en":"<b>трамва́й</b>","gloss":"[tram-VAY]","say":"трамвай"}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","q":"Spell “museum” in Russian (sound it out: m-oo-z-ye-y):","answer":"м у з е й"},{"type":"choice","q":"Read the whole sentence: <b>Э́то трамва́й.</b> What did you just say?","opts":["This is a museum.","This is a tram.","Where is the tram?"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Э́то трамва́й</b> = “This is a tram.” A full sentence, read by you — that's real Russian."},{"type":"speak","target":"трамва́й","sub":"[tram-VAY] — end it with one quick glide, not a stretched vowel","want":"трамвай"}]}],"vocab":{"intro":"Today's words — including the tiniest one in the whole language. Flip a card to see the sound and the meaning. Every word uses only letters you've already met.","items":[{"en":"и","ru":"and","ex":"[ee] — ма́ма и па́па (mum and dad)"},{"en":"э́то","ru":"this / this is","ex":"[E-to] — Э́то до́м. (This is a house.)"},{"en":"зима́","ru":"winter","ex":"[zee-MA] — the cold season"},{"en":"фла́г","ru":"flag","ex":"[flag] — a flag"},{"en":"э́хо","ru":"echo","ex":"[E-kho] — an echo"},{"en":"музе́й","ru":"museum","ex":"[moo-ZYEY] — Э́то музе́й. (This is a museum.)"},{"en":"трамва́й","ru":"tram","ex":"[tram-VAY] — a tram"},{"en":"ми́р","ru":"world; peace","ex":"[meer] — one word, two lovely meanings"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Drag each letter to the sound it makes. Today's four are here — plus three from yesterday, just to keep them warm.","pairs":[["И","ee (see)"],["Й","y (boy — a short glide)"],["Ф","f (fun)"],["Э","e (end)"],["Б","b (book)"],["З","z (zoo)"],["Л","l (lamp)"]]},"reading":{"intro":"Read these aloud, slowly. Tap 🔊 to hear them. The last two lines are complete sentences — your first ones. Take a breath and enjoy that.","title":"Чита́ем! (Let's read)","sentences":["и","э́то","зима́","фла́г","э́хо","музе́й","трамва́й","Э́то до́м.","Э́то музе́й."],"translation":"and · this · winter · flag · echo · museum · tram · This is a house. · This is a museum."},"quiz":{"intro":"Last stretch — five quick checks and the whole “new shapes” family is yours. You've got this. 💛","items":[{"q":"1. Which sound does <b>И</b> make?","opts":["f (as in “fun”)","ee (as in “see”)","y (as in “boy” — a short glide)"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>И</b> = <b>ee</b>, a full vowel. The short glide belongs to its twin with the hat — <b>Й</b>."},{"q":"2. What's the real difference between <b>И</b> and <b>Й</b>?","opts":["И is a full vowel; Й is a quick y-glide, like the y in “boy”","they sound exactly the same","Й is silent — it's only decoration"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>И</b> you can stretch — “eeee”. <b>Й</b> (“short И”) is a glide, over in an instant: <b>трамва́й</b>, <b>музе́й</b>."},{"q":"3. Read it: <b>э́то</b> means —","opts":["echo","winter","this / this is"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>э́то</b> = <b>this</b>. Careful with its look-alike <b>э́хо</b> (echo) — one letter apart, worlds apart."},{"q":"4. Which word means “tram”?","opts":["музе́й","трамва́й","фла́г"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>трамва́й</b> = tram. <b>музе́й</b> = museum, <b>фла́г</b> = flag — you read all three today."},{"q":"5. Which sound does <b>Э</b> make?","opts":["ye (as in “yes”)","e (as in “end”)","a (as in “father”)"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Э</b> is the plain <b>e</b> of “end”. The “ye” sound belongs to <b>Е</b> from Lesson 2 — the trap you already know how to dodge."}]}},
"5": {"day":5,"week":"01","themeRu":"The hushers","themeEn":"Ж Ц Ч Ш Щ","intro":"Today is a milestone: five letters that don't look like anything in your alphabet. That sounds like the hard part — it's actually the best news so far, because your eye has no wrong habit to unlearn. Four of them are the famous “hushers” — hissing, rustling sounds your mouth already knows how to make — plus one crisp little firecracker of a sound. By the end of this lesson you'll be reading the name of the most famous soup in the world. Let's meet them. 💛","introAudio":"Today is a milestone: five letters that don't look like anything in your alphabet. That sounds like the hard part, but it's actually the best news so far, because your eye has no wrong habit to unlearn. Four of them are the famous hushers — hissing, rustling sounds your mouth already knows how to make — plus one crisp little firecracker of a sound. By the end of this lesson you'll be reading the name of the most famous soup in the world. Let's meet them.","goals":["Recognise five brand-new letters and their sounds: <b>zh, ts, ch, sh, shch</b>","Tell the two hushing look-alikes apart — the plain one and the long soft one","Sound out real words like <b>ча́й</b> (tea) and <b>бо́рщ</b> (borscht)","Read eight real Russian words aloud with the new letters"],"learned":["Recognised five brand-new letters — <b>Ж, Ц, Ч, Ш, Щ</b>","Learned to tell <b>Ш</b> (plain “sh”) from <b>Щ</b> (long soft “shch”)","Sounded out real words — <b>ча́й</b> (tea) and <b>борщ</b> (borscht)","Read eight real Russian words aloud with the new letters"],"review":{"intro":"Quick warm-up first — yesterday's four letters, still fresh. Thirty seconds of looking back makes today's new sounds land better.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just your brain taking notes.","q":"Which sound does the letter <b>И</b> make?","opts":["ee (as in “see”)","y (as in “boy”)","e (as in “met”)"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>И</b> = the long <b>ee</b> of “see”. Its short cousin <b>Й</b> is the quick “y” glide at the end of “boy”."},{"type":"choice","q":"Read it: <b>э́то</b> means —","opts":["museum","this","winter"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>э́то</b> = <b>this</b> — one of the most used words in all of Russian. You'll see it everywhere."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “museum”?","opts":["трамва́й","зима́","музе́й"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>музе́й</b> [moo-ZYEY] = museum. <b>трамва́й</b> = tram, <b>зима́</b> = winter — and notice how <b>музе́й</b> and <b>трамва́й</b> both end in that short <b>й</b>."}],"introAudio":"Quick warm-up first — yesterday's four letters, still fresh. Thirty seconds of looking back makes today's new sounds land better."},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Letters 1 · Brand-new sounds","title":"Five letters with <em>no English twin</em>","paras":["These five look like nothing in English — and that's their gift: your eye has no wrong instinct to fight, the way it did with <b>Р</b> and <b>С</b>. Each one stands for a sound English usually needs two or three letters to spell. Russian gives that sound its own single letter.","Tap the 🔊 on each one and listen closely. Say it back softly and feel where the air hisses. That's the whole job right now."],"audio":"These five look like nothing in English, and that's their gift: your eye has no wrong instinct to fight. Each one stands for a sound English usually needs two or three letters to spell. Russian gives that sound its own single letter. Tap the speaker on each one and listen closely. Say it back softly and feel where the air hisses.","table":{"rows":[["Ж <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"жэ\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>zh</b> — the “s” in “plea<b>s</b>ure”"],["Ц <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"цэ\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>ts</b> in “ca<b>ts</b>” — one crisp sound"],["Ч <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"че\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>ch</b> in “<b>ch</b>at”"],["Ш <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ша\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>sh</b> in “<b>sh</b>op” — plain and hard"],["Щ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ща\">🔊</button>","a long, soft <b>shch</b> — like “fre<b>sh sh</b>eets” said quickly"]]},"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. Wrong taps are part of the learning.","q":"Which sound does the letter <b>Ж</b> make?","opts":["j (as in “jam”)","zh (the “s” in “pleasure”)","g (as in “go”)"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Ж</b> = <b>zh</b> — the buzzing middle of “plea<b>s</b>ure” or “vi<b>s</b>ion”. English has this sound; it just never gave it a letter of its own."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which sound does the letter <b>Ц</b> make?","opts":["s (as in “sun”)","k (as in “kite”)","ts (as in “ca<b>ts</b>”)"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>Ц</b> = <b>ts</b> — like the end of “cats”, said as one crisp sound. Russian can even start a word with it: <b>ци́рк</b> (circus)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Letters 2 · The look-alike pair","title":"<em>Ш</em> or <em>Щ</em>? Spot the tail","paras":["<b>Ш</b> and <b>Щ</b> are the pair everyone mixes up at first — same three prongs, but <b>Щ</b> has a little tail at the bottom. The tail changes the sound: <b>Ш</b> is a plain, hard <b>sh</b> as in “shop”; <b>Щ</b> is longer and softer — smile slightly and stretch it, <b>shsh</b>.","Hear them side by side: <b>ду́ш</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"душ\">🔊</button> (shower) ends in the plain <b>Ш</b>; <b>борщ</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"борщ\">🔊</button> (borscht) ends in the long soft <b>Щ</b>. Say both out loud and feel the difference in your mouth."],"audio":"Ш and Щ are the pair everyone mixes up at first: same three prongs, but Щ has a little tail at the bottom. The tail changes the sound. Ш is a plain, hard sh, as in shop. Щ is longer and softer: smile slightly and stretch it. Hear them side by side: душ, shower, ends in the plain Ш. борщ, borscht, ends in the long soft Щ. Say both out loud and feel the difference.","examples":[{"ru":"shower","en":"<b>ду́ш</b>","gloss":"[doosh]","say":"душ"},{"ru":"borscht (beet soup)","en":"<b>борщ</b>","gloss":"[borshch]","say":"борщ"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You hear a long, soft, stretched “shsh”. Which letter is it?","opts":["Ш","Щ","Ц"],"answer":1,"explain":"The long soft one is <b>Щ</b> — the one with the tail. <b>Ш</b> is short and hard, and <b>Ц</b> is the crisp “ts”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the letters in the right order to spell the word.","q":"Spell “shower” in Russian (say the sounds as you tap: d-oo-sh):","answer":"д у ш"}]},{"eyebrow":"Reading 1 · Real words","title":"Read your first <em>husher words</em>","paras":["Time to put the new letters to work. Sound each word out slowly, letter by letter, then say it again at full speed. The little stress mark still shows you the loud part.","Watch <b>ча́й</b> and <b>ча́с</b> — one letter apart, two very different things: tea and an hour. Russian rewards a careful eye."],"audio":"Time to put the new letters to work. Sound each word out slowly, letter by letter, then say it again at full speed. The little stress mark still shows you the loud part. Watch чай and час: one letter apart, two very different things, tea and an hour.","examples":[{"ru":"beetle","en":"<b>жу́к</b>","gloss":"[zhook]","say":"жук"},{"ru":"tea","en":"<b>ча́й</b>","gloss":"[chay]","say":"чай"},{"ru":"school","en":"<b>шко́ла</b>","gloss":"[SHKO-la]","say":"школа"},{"ru":"street","en":"<b>у́лица</b>","gloss":"[OO-lee-tsa]","say":"улица"}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the letters in the right order to spell the word.","q":"Spell “tea” in Russian (say the sounds as you tap: ch-a-y):","answer":"ч а й"},{"type":"choice","q":"Read it aloud: <b>ча́с</b>. What does it mean?","opts":["tea","hour","beetle"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ча́с</b> = <b>hour</b>. Its near-twin <b>ча́й</b> = tea — only the last letter differs, so read all the way to the end."},{"type":"speak","target":"шко́ла","sub":"[SHKO-la] — say the first part a little louder","want":"школа"}]}],"vocab":{"intro":"Eight real words, all built from today's new letters plus your old ones. Flip a card to see the sound and the meaning.","items":[{"en":"жу́к","ru":"beetle","ex":"[zhook] — a beetle"},{"en":"ча́й","ru":"tea","ex":"[chay] — a cup of tea"},{"en":"ча́с","ru":"hour","ex":"[chas] — one hour"},{"en":"ду́ш","ru":"shower","ex":"[doosh] — a shower"},{"en":"шко́ла","ru":"school","ex":"[SHKO-la] — a school"},{"en":"бо́рщ","ru":"borscht (beet soup)","ex":"[borshch] — the famous beet soup"},{"en":"ци́рк","ru":"circus","ex":"[tsyrk] — a circus"},{"en":"у́лица","ru":"street","ex":"[OO-lee-tsa] — a street"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Quick memory game. Drag each new letter to the sound it makes.","pairs":[["Ж","zh (the “s” in “pleasure”)"],["Ц","ts (cats)"],["Ч","ch (chat)"],["Ш","sh (shop) — plain and hard"],["Щ","shch — long and soft"]]},"reading":{"intro":"Read these real words aloud, slowly at first. Tap 🔊 to hear each one. Every word here uses only letters you already know.","title":"Чита́ем! (Let's read)","sentences":["жу́к","ча́й","ча́с","ду́ш","шко́ла","бо́рщ","ци́рк","у́лица"],"translation":"beetle · tea · hour · shower · school · borscht · circus · street"},"quiz":{"intro":"Last stretch — five quick checks and the hushers are yours. 💛","items":[{"q":"1. Which sound does <b>Ж</b> make?","opts":["zh — the “s” in “pleasure”","j — as in “jam”","z — as in “zoo”"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Ж</b> = <b>zh</b>. English hides this sound inside words like “measure” and “vision”; Russian gave it its own letter."},{"q":"2. Read it: <b>ча́й</b> means —","opts":["hour","tea","circus"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ча́й</b> = <b>tea</b>. Careful with its near-twin <b>ча́с</b> (hour) — the last letter decides the meaning."},{"q":"3. Which word means “school”?","opts":["у́лица","шко́ла","ци́рк"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>шко́ла</b> = school — it even sounds like it. <b>у́лица</b> = street, <b>ци́рк</b> = circus."},{"q":"4. What is the difference between <b>Ш</b> and <b>Щ</b>?","opts":["<b>Ш</b> is a plain hard “sh”; <b>Щ</b> is longer and softer","They sound exactly the same","<b>Щ</b> makes no sound at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"The tail on <b>Щ</b> means a longer, softer sound — “fresh sheets” said quickly. <b>Ш</b> is the short, hard “sh” of “shop”."},{"q":"5. <b>борщ</b> ends in <b>Щ</b>. How does that ending sound?","opts":["a plain short “sh”","a hard “k”","a long soft “shch”"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>борщ</b> = [borshch] — the ending stretches out, long and soft. You can now read the most famous item on the menu. 🍲"}]}},
"6": {"day":6,"week":"01","themeRu":"Vowels & signs","themeEn":"Ю Я Ё · Ь Ъ Ы","intro":"Today is a milestone day: you finish the alphabet. Six letters to go — three friendly “two-in-one” vowels, then the three oddballs: a sign that softens, a sign that separates, and a vowel English simply doesn't have. Two of them make no sound at all — I'll explain everything. By the end of this lesson you'll know all 33 Russian letters. All of them. 💛","introAudio":"Today is a milestone day: you finish the alphabet. Six letters to go. Three friendly two-in-one vowels, then the three oddballs: a sign that softens, a sign that separates, and a vowel English simply doesn't have. Two of them make no sound at all. I'll explain everything. By the end of this lesson you'll know all thirty-three Russian letters. All of them.","goals":["Read the last three vowels — <b>Ю, Я, Ё</b> — as two-in-one sounds","Learn what the silent signs <b>Ь</b> and <b>Ъ</b> really do","Meet <b>Ы</b> — the vowel English doesn't have — and hear how it differs from <b>И</b>","Complete all 33 letters and read real words like <b>семья́</b> (family)"],"learned":["Read the last three vowels — <b>Ю, Я, Ё</b> — and learned that ё is always stressed","Discovered that <b>Ь</b> and <b>Ъ</b> make no sound of their own — they quietly shape the letters around them","Heard and tried <b>Ы</b> — the deep vowel English doesn't have","Finished all 33 letters of the Russian alphabet and read <b>семья́</b> (family) aloud"],"review":{"intro":"One quick look back before the finale. Yesterday you met the hissing letters — Ж, Ц, Ч, Ш, Щ. Prove they stuck, then we'll finish the alphabet.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. Wrong taps are totally fine — that's how it sticks.","q":"Which sound does the letter <b>Ж</b> make?","opts":["zh (like the s in “pleasure”)","z (as in “zoo”)","g (as in “go”)"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Ж</b> = <b>zh</b>, the buzzing sound in “plea<b>s</b>ure”. Plain <b>z</b> belongs to <b>З</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"Read it aloud: <b>ча́й</b>. What does it mean?","opts":["hour","shower","tea"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>ча́й</b> [chay] = <b>tea</b>. (<b>ча́с</b> is hour and <b>ду́ш</b> is shower — all husher words from yesterday.)"},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the letters in the right order to spell the word.","q":"Spell “school” in Russian (say the sounds as you tap: sh-k-o-l-a):","answer":"ш к о л а"}],"introAudio":"One quick look back before the finale. Yesterday you met the hissing letters — Ж, Ц, Ч, Ш, Щ. Prove they stuck, then we'll finish the alphabet."},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Letters 1 · Two-in-one vowels","title":"Ю, Я, Ё — <em>two sounds in one letter</em>","paras":["Three vowels to finish the set — and they're friendly. Each one is really two sounds glued together: a quick English “y” plus a vowel you already know. <b>Я</b> = [ya] as in “yard”, <b>Ю</b> = [yu] as in “you”, <b>Ё</b> = [yo] as in “yolk”. Tap the 🔊 on each one and say it back softly.","<b>Ё</b> comes with a gift: it is <b>always</b> the stressed syllable of its word. That's why you'll never see a stress mark on it — the two dots already tell you where the loud part is. And <b>я</b> on its own is a whole word: <b>я</b> means “I”."],"audio":"Three vowels to finish the set, and they're friendly. Each one is really two sounds glued together: a quick y plus a vowel you already know. Я sounds like ya in yard. Ю sounds like yu in you. Ё sounds like yo in yolk. And ё comes with a gift: it is always the stressed syllable of its word, so it never needs a stress mark. One more thing: я on its own is a whole word. Я means I.","table":{"rows":[["Я <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>ya</b> in “yard” — alone, it's the word <b>я</b> (I)"],["Ю <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ю\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>yu</b> in “you”"],["Ё <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ё\">🔊</button>","sounds like <b>yo</b> in “yolk” — and it is <b>always</b> stressed"]]},"examples":[{"ru":"I","en":"<b>я</b>","gloss":"[ya]","say":"я"},{"ru":"apple","en":"<b>я́блоко</b>","gloss":"[YA-blo-ko]","say":"яблоко"},{"ru":"south","en":"<b>ю́г</b>","gloss":"[yug]","say":"юг"},{"ru":"hedgehog","en":"<b>ёж</b>","gloss":"[yozh]","say":"ёж"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The letter <b>я</b> at the start of a word — one sound or two?","opts":["One — just “a”","Two — a quick “y” plus “a”, like in “yard”","One — just “y”"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>я</b> = <b>y + a</b>, two sounds packed into one letter. Same trick as ю [yu] and ё [yo]."},{"type":"choice","q":"Where is the stress in <b>ёлка</b> (fir-tree)?","opts":["On the ё — ё is always stressed","On the а","You can't tell without a dictionary"],"answer":0,"explain":"If a word has <b>ё</b>, the stress lives there — every single time. That's why ё never needs the little accent mark."}]},{"eyebrow":"Letters 2 · The silent signs","title":"Ь and Ъ — <em>letters with no sound</em>","paras":["Now the strangest pair in the alphabet, and I'll be honest with you: <b>ь</b> (the soft sign) and <b>ъ</b> (the hard sign) make <b>no sound at all</b>. None. They're not vowels, they're not consonants — they're tiny written instructions about the letter next door.","<b>Ь</b> softens the consonant before it — say the sound and let a whisper of “y” melt into it. Compare <b>мат</b> [mat] (checkmate) with <b>ма́ть</b> [mat'] (mother): that tiny softness changes the whole word. In <b>семья́</b> [syem-YA] (family), the ь keeps the м and the я politely apart. <b>Ъ</b> is much rarer — a small separator inside a few words, like <b>подъе́зд</b> (entrance). For today, just recognise the pair; the full soft-and-hard story comes in Lesson 10."],"audio":"Now the strangest pair in the alphabet, and I'll be honest with you: the soft sign and the hard sign make no sound at all. None. They're tiny written instructions about the letter next door. The soft sign softens the consonant before it. Compare мат with мать: that tiny softness changes the whole word. The hard sign is much rarer, a small separator inside a few words. For today, just recognise the pair. The full soft-and-hard story comes in Lesson 10.","examples":[{"ru":"mother","en":"<b>ма́ть</b>","gloss":"[mat'] — the ь softens the т","say":"мать"},{"ru":"family","en":"<b>семья́</b>","gloss":"[syem-YA] — the ь separates м and я","say":"семья"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"What sound does <b>ь</b> (the soft sign) make on its own?","opts":["A quiet “b”","None — it softens the consonant before it","“Y” as in “yes”"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ь</b> is silent — its whole job is to soften the consonant right before it. ма́ть ends in a soft т, мат in a plain one."},{"type":"build","q":"Spell “mother” in Russian (m-a-t + the soft sign):","answer":"м а т ь"},{"type":"build","q":"Now the show-off word — spell “family” (the ь sits right in the middle):","answer":"с е м ь я"}]},{"eyebrow":"Letters 3 · The stranger","title":"Ы — <em>the vowel English doesn't have</em>","paras":["One letter left in the whole alphabet — and it's the tricky one. <b>Ы</b> is a vowel English simply doesn't have. Start saying “ee”, then pull your tongue back and down, deeper into your mouth. Listen first: <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ы\">🔊</button> — it will sound a little alien, and that's completely normal.","Compare it with <b>и</b>: и is a bright, forward “ee”; ы is its deeper, darker cousin. Try <b>мы</b> (we), <b>ты</b> (you), <b>сы́р</b> (cheese) — and don't chase perfection. Today the win is <em>hearing</em> the difference between ы and и; your mouth will catch up in Lesson 10, with practice."],"audio":"One letter left in the whole alphabet, and it's the tricky one. Ы is a vowel English simply doesn't have. Start saying ee, then pull your tongue back and down, deeper into your mouth. It will sound a little alien, and that's completely normal. Compare it with и: и is a bright, forward ee; ы is its deeper, darker cousin. Try мы, ты, сыр, and don't chase perfection. Today the win is hearing the difference. Your mouth will catch up with practice.","examples":[{"ru":"we","en":"<b>мы</b>","gloss":"[my]","say":"мы"},{"ru":"you (one person)","en":"<b>ты</b>","gloss":"[ty]","say":"ты"},{"ru":"cheese","en":"<b>сы́р</b>","gloss":"[syr]","say":"сыр"},{"ru":"fish","en":"<b>ры́ба</b>","gloss":"[RY-ba]","say":"рыба"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which of these describes <b>ы</b>?","opts":["Exactly the same as и","Silent, like ь","A deep “i” — like “ee” but with the tongue pulled back"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>ы</b> is the deep “i” said further back in the mouth. It's genuinely new for an English speaker — give it time."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the meaning in English.","q":"You read <b>мы</b>. Write what it means in English:","accept":["we"],"placeholder":"in English…"},{"type":"speak","target":"сы́р","sub":"[syr] — deep “i”, tongue pulled back","want":"сыр"}]}],"vocab":{"intro":"The last new words of the letters stage. Flip each card for the sound and the meaning — and notice the two-in-one vowels and the soft sign doing their quiet work.","items":[{"en":"я","ru":"I","ex":"[ya] — the shortest Russian word"},{"en":"я́блоко","ru":"apple","ex":"[YA-blo-ko] — an apple"},{"en":"ю́г","ru":"south","ex":"[yug] — the south"},{"en":"ёж","ru":"hedgehog","ex":"[yozh] — a hedgehog"},{"en":"ёлка","ru":"fir-tree","ex":"[YOL-ka] — a Christmas tree"},{"en":"сы́р","ru":"cheese","ex":"[syr] — cheese"},{"en":"ры́ба","ru":"fish","ex":"[RY-ba] — a fish"},{"en":"семья́","ru":"family","ex":"[syem-YA] — a family"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match game, final edition. Drag each letter to what it does. Two of these make no sound at all — you know which ones.","pairs":[["Я","ya (yard)"],["Ю","yu (you)"],["Ё","yo (yolk) — always stressed"],["Ы","deep “i” — tongue pulled back"],["Ь","no sound — softens the letter before it"],["Ъ","no sound — a rare separator"]]},"reading":{"intro":"Read each word aloud, slowly. Tap 🔊 to hear it first if you like. Every single letter of the Russian alphabet is now a letter you know.","title":"Чита́ем! (Let's read)","sentences":["я","я́блоко","ю́г","ёж","ёлка","мы́","ты́","сы́р","ры́ба","ма́ть","семья́"],"translation":"I · apple · south · hedgehog · fir-tree · we · you (one person) · cheese · fish · mother · family"},"quiz":{"intro":"Last checks of the day — and the last letters of the alphabet. After this you can look at any Russian word and name every letter in it. That deserves a small celebration. 💛","items":[{"q":"1. Which letter is <b>always</b> the stressed part of its word?","opts":["ё","а","ы"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only <b>ё</b>. If a word has ё, the stress lives there — that's why ё never carries an extra accent mark."},{"q":"2. What sound does <b>ь</b> (the soft sign) make on its own?","opts":["A quiet “b”","“Ya” — y + a","None — it softens the consonant before it"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>ь</b> has no sound of its own — it melts a hint of softness into the consonant before it. (“Ya” is <b>я</b>'s job.)"},{"q":"3. Which word means “mother”?","opts":["мат","ма́ть","ты́"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ма́ть</b> = mother. Without the soft sign, <b>мат</b> is a different word — “checkmate” in chess. One silent letter, two different words."},{"q":"4. Which vowel has no English twin — the deep “i” said with the tongue pulled back?","opts":["ы","и","ю"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>ы</b> — the deep “i”. <b>и</b> is the bright, forward “ee”, and <b>ю</b> is the two-in-one “yu”."},{"q":"5. Read it: <b>семья́</b> means —","opts":["family","seven","cheese"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>семья́</b> [syem-YA] = <b>family</b>. You read it with a soft sign in the middle — all 33 letters are yours now."}]}},
"7": {"day":7,"week":"01","themeRu":"Review · the whole alphabet","themeEn":"Весь алфави́т","intro":"Stop and look back for a moment: six lessons ago, Cyrillic was a wall of strange shapes. Today you know every single letter — all 33 of them. That is not a small thing; most people never get this far. So this lesson is a victory lap: we retell the five groups as one story, lay the whole alphabet out on one page, and then try something new — reading words you've never seen before, completely cold. You're ready for it. 💛","introAudio":"Stop and look back for a moment. Six lessons ago, Cyrillic was a wall of strange shapes. Today you know every single letter, all thirty-three of them. That is not a small thing. So this lesson is a victory lap: we retell the five groups as one story, lay the whole alphabet out on one page, and then try something new: reading words you've never seen before, completely cold. You're ready for it.","goals":["Retell the five letter groups — true friends, false friends, new shapes, brand-new sounds, vowels and signs — as one story","See the whole 33-letter alphabet laid out in dictionary order for the first time","Learn a calm three-step plan for reading a word you have never seen","Read a mixed set of brand-new words cold — airplane, shop, thank you, coffee, good — plus a victory lap of old favourites"],"learned":["Retold the five letter groups as one story","Met the full 33-letter alphabet in dictionary order","Learned the three-step plan for cold reading: find the vowels, chunk the word, say it slowly","Read brand-new words cold — airplane, shop, thank you, coffee, good — plus a victory lap from lessons 1–6"],"review":{"intro":"Before the celebration — a quick warm-up that jumps across the groups. Every answer is something you've already met; wrong taps just show us where to look back.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is a working moment, not a mistake.","q":"Lesson 2 flashback — which sound does <b>С</b> make?","opts":["k (as in “kite”)","s (as in “sun”)","ts (as in “cats”)"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>С</b> is a false friend: it looks like English C but always says <b>s</b>. The [ts] sound belongs to <b>Ц</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"Lesson 3 flashback — which sound does <b>Б</b> make?","opts":["b (as in “book”)","v (as in “voice”)","d (as in “day”)"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Б</b> = <b>b</b>. Don't mix it up with <b>В</b>, the false friend that looks like B but says <b>v</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"Lesson 5 flashback — which letter makes the [ch] sound, as in “chat”?","opts":["Ц","Ш","Ч"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>Ч</b> = <b>ch</b>. <b>Ц</b> is [ts] and <b>Ш</b> is [sh] — three different mouths, three different letters."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the answer in Latin letters.","q":"Lesson 6 flashback — at the start of a word, <b>я</b> makes two sounds glued together. Type its romanization (two Latin letters):","accept":["ya"],"placeholder":"two letters…"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Review 1 · The five groups","title":"The five groups — <em>one story</em>","paras":["Here is your whole journey in one story. You started with five letters that were exactly what they seemed — the <b>true friends</b>. Then came seven that lie to an English eye: same shape, different sound — the <b>false friends</b>. Then ten honest <b>new shapes</b> carrying sounds you already owned. Then five letters for the sounds English spells with letter-teams — <b>zh, ts, ch, sh, shch</b>. And finally the last six: three friendly y-vowels, the deep <b>ы</b>, and two signs that make no sound at all.","Read the table below like a story, not a test. If one row feels foggy, that's useful information — you know exactly which lesson to glance back at. Everything else today mixes these groups together."],"audio":"Here is your whole journey in one story. You started with five letters that were exactly what they seemed, the true friends. Then came seven that lie to an English eye: same shape, different sound, the false friends. Then ten honest new shapes carrying sounds you already owned. Then five letters for the sounds English spells with letter teams: zh, ts, ch, sh, shch. And finally the last six: three friendly y-vowels, the deep vowel ы, and two signs that make no sound at all. Read the table like a story, not a test.","table":{"rows":[["<b>True friends</b> (Lesson 1)","А К М О Т — look English, sound English"],["<b>False friends</b> (Lesson 2)","В Е Н Р С У Х — look English, sound Russian"],["<b>New shapes</b> (Lessons 3–4)","Б Г Д З Л П И Й Ф Э — new look, familiar sounds"],["<b>Brand-new sounds</b> (Lesson 5)","Ж Ц Ч Ш Щ — zh · ts · ch · sh · shch"],["<b>Vowels &amp; signs</b> (Lesson 6)","Ю Я Ё Ы Ь Ъ — the y-vowels, the deep ы, and two silent signs"]]},"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which group does <b>Н</b> belong to?","opts":["False friends — it looks like English H but says n","True friends — it looks and sounds like English","Brand-new sounds — English has nothing like it"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Н</b> is the classic false friend: your eye sees H, but the sound is <b>n</b>, as in “nose” — но́с."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which of these three letters is a false friend (looks English, sounds different)?","opts":["М","Р","Ф"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Р</b> looks like P but says a rolled <b>r</b>. <b>М</b> is a true friend (m), and <b>Ф</b> is a new shape for a familiar sound (f)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Review 2 · А to Я","title":"The whole alphabet — <em>in order, on one page</em>","paras":["You learned the letters by family, because that's how they stick. But Russian files its words the plain way — а, б, в, г… — in dictionaries, phone contacts, indexes, lists. Here is the official order, all 33 letters. Look at it slowly: not one of these shapes is a stranger to you any more.","You don't need to memorise the order today — it will matter later, when you start looking words up. For now, tap 🔊 on a row to hear the letter names, and enjoy the view."],"audio":"You learned the letters by family, because that's how they stick. But Russian files its words the plain way, a, b, v, g, in dictionaries, phone contacts, indexes, lists. Here is the official order, all thirty-three letters. Look at it slowly: not one of these shapes is a stranger to you any more. You don't need to memorise the order today. Tap the speaker on a row to hear the letter names, and enjoy the view.","table":{"rows":[["А Б В Г Д Е <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"а, бэ, вэ, гэ, дэ, е\">🔊</button>","a · b · v · g · d · ye"],["Ё Ж З И Й К <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ё, жэ, зэ, и, и краткое, ка\">🔊</button>","yo · zh · z · ee · y · k"],["Л М Н О П Р <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"эль, эм, эн, о, пэ, эр\">🔊</button>","l · m · n · o · p · r"],["С Т У Ф Х Ц <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"эс, тэ, у, эф, ха, цэ\">🔊</button>","s · t · oo · f · kh · ts"],["Ч Ш Щ Ъ Ы Ь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"че, ша, ща, твёрдый знак, ы, мягкий знак\">🔊</button>","ch · sh · shch · (hard sign) · y · (soft sign)"],["Э Ю Я <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"э, ю, я\">🔊</button>","e · yu · ya"]]},"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Dictionary order — which of these words would come <b>first</b>?","opts":["ко́т","а́том","му́ха"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>а́том</b> starts with <b>а</b> — the very first letter of the alphabet. <b>к</b> and <b>м</b> come later in the row."}]},{"eyebrow":"Review 3 · Cold reading","title":"How to read a word <em>you've never seen</em>","paras":["Now the new skill: reading cold. The plan has three steps. <b>One</b> — find the vowels; every vowel is one beat of the word. <b>Two</b> — chunk the word around them: самолёт → са-мо-лёт. <b>Three</b> — say the chunks slowly, then glue them together, saying the marked chunk a little louder. (Remember: <b>ё</b> is always the loud one, so it never needs a mark.)","Slow is the whole trick. Every reader of Russian — including every Russian child — started by crawling through words one chunk at a time. Speed arrives on its own; your only job is to keep the sounds honest."],"audio":"Now the new skill: reading cold. The plan has three steps. One: find the vowels; every vowel is one beat of the word. Two: chunk the word around them: самолёт becomes са, мо, лёт. Three: say the chunks slowly, then glue them together, saying the marked chunk a little louder. Slow is the whole trick. Every reader of Russian started by crawling through words one chunk at a time. Speed arrives on its own; your only job is to keep the sounds honest. And remember: ё is always the loud syllable, that is why it never needs a stress mark.","examples":[{"ru":"airplane","en":"<b>самолёт</b>","gloss":"[sa-mo-LYOT] — са-мо-лёт","say":"самолёт"},{"ru":"shop, store","en":"<b>магази́н</b>","gloss":"[ma-ga-ZEEN] — ма-га-зи́н","say":"магазин"},{"ru":"thank you","en":"<b>спаси́бо</b>","gloss":"[spa-SEE-bo] — спа-си́-бо","say":"спасибо"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Chunk it like a reader: <b>магази́н</b> breaks into —","opts":["ма-га-зи́н","маг-ази́н","м-а-г-а-з-и-н"],"answer":0,"explain":"Three vowels (а, а, и) mean three chunks: <b>ма-га-зи́н</b>, with the last chunk said louder. Single letters aren't chunks — vowels set the beat."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the letters in the right order to spell the word.","q":"Spell “coffee” in Russian — ко́фе [KO-fye]:","answer":"к о ф е"},{"type":"speak","target":"хорошо́","sub":"[kho-ro-SHO] — three chunks, the last one louder","want":"хорошо"}]}],"vocab":{"intro":"Your victory-lap words. Five are brand-new — you just read them cold — and three are old friends from earlier lessons. Flip each card to check the sound and the meaning.","items":[{"en":"самолёт","ru":"airplane","ex":"[sa-mo-LYOT] — a plane in the sky"},{"en":"магази́н","ru":"shop, store","ex":"[ma-ga-ZEEN] — a shop"},{"en":"спаси́бо","ru":"thank you","ex":"[spa-SEE-bo] — thanks"},{"en":"ко́фе","ru":"coffee","ex":"[KO-fye] — a cup of coffee"},{"en":"хорошо́","ru":"good, well, OK","ex":"[kho-ro-SHO] — all good"},{"en":"ча́й","ru":"tea","ex":"[chay] — from lesson 5"},{"en":"семья́","ru":"family","ex":"[syem-YA] — from lesson 6"},{"en":"ры́ба","ru":"fish","ex":"[RY-ba] — from lesson 6"}]},"drag":{"intro":"The final letter game of Stage 1. These seven letters come from all five groups — drag each one to the sound it makes.","pairs":[["А","a (father)"],["Р","r (rolled r)"],["Х","kh (Scottish “loch”)"],["Д","d (day)"],["Щ","shch (“fresh sheets”)"],["Ю","yu (you)"],["Ы","y (deep, hard “i”)"]]},"reading":{"intro":"The moment this whole stage was building to. Read these aloud, slowly, chunk by chunk. The first five are brand-new — cold reading. The rest are your victory lap from lessons 1–6. Tap 🔊 to check yourself.","title":"Чита́ем всё! (We read everything)","sentences":["самолёт","магази́н","спаси́бо","ко́фе","хорошо́","ко́т","но́с","дру́г","музе́й","бо́рщ","семья́"],"translation":"airplane · shop · thank you · coffee · good/well · cat · nose · friend · museum · borscht · family"},"quiz":{"intro":"The last check of Stage 1 — six questions across all five groups. After this, the whole alphabet is officially yours. 💛","items":[{"q":"1. The letter <b>В</b> looks like English B. Which sound does it really make?","opts":["b (as in “book”)","v (as in “voice”)","w (as in “win”)"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>В</b> is the classic false friend — it always says <b>v</b>. The [b] sound belongs to <b>Б</b>, and Russian has no [w] at all."},{"q":"2. Which sound does <b>Ы</b> make?","opts":["ee (as in “see”)","no sound at all","a deep, hard “i” said far back in the mouth"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>Ы</b> is the vowel English doesn't have — a hard “i” from deep in the mouth, as in <b>ры́ба</b>. The bright [ee] is <b>И</b>, and “no sound” describes the signs, not a vowel."},{"q":"3. How much sound do <b>ь</b> and <b>ъ</b> make on their own?","opts":["None — they only change how the letters around them are read","A short “uh”","y (as in “yes”)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The soft sign <b>ь</b> and the hard sign <b>ъ</b> are silent workers: <b>ь</b> softens the consonant before it, <b>ъ</b> keeps a consonant and a y-vowel apart. Zero sound of their own."},{"q":"4. Which letter says [zh], like the s in “measure”?","opts":["Х","Ж","Ц"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Ж</b> = <b>zh</b> — the buzzing beetle from <b>жу́к</b>. <b>Х</b> is [kh] and <b>Ц</b> is [ts]."},{"q":"5. Read it cold: <b>хорошо́</b> means —","opts":["thank you","shop","good / well"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>хорошо́</b> [kho-ro-SHO] = <b>good / well</b> — one of the most-used words in Russian, and you just read it unseen."},{"q":"6. Which sound does <b>Г</b> make?","opts":["g (as in “go”)","r (rolled)","kh (as in “loch”)"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Г</b> = <b>g</b>, as in <b>го́род</b> (city). The rolled [r] is <b>Р</b>, and [kh] is <b>Х</b>."}]}},
"8": {"day":8,"week":"02","themeRu":"Stress","themeEn":"Ударе́ние","intro":"You did it — all 33 letters are yours, and that was the hard climb. From today we work on something new: not <b>what</b> the letters are, but how a whole word actually sounds. And the first thing to master is stress — the one loud syllable every Russian word has. Get the stress right, and people will understand you even when everything else wobbles. Get it wrong, and sometimes you're saying a completely different word. Let's train your ear. 💛","introAudio":"You did it — all 33 letters are yours, and that was the hard climb. From today we work on something new: not what the letters are, but how a whole word actually sounds. And the first thing to master is stress, the one loud syllable every Russian word has. Get the stress right, and people will understand you even when everything else wobbles. Get it wrong, and sometimes you're saying a completely different word. Let's train your ear.","goals":["Feel what Russian stress is — every word has exactly <b>one</b> loud syllable","Spot the stressed syllable by ear and by the little mark we print for you","Hear how moving the stress can flip a word's meaning — castle [ZA-mok] vs lock [za-MOK]","Read five everyday words with the right stress: window, dog, well, thank you, please"],"learned":["Felt what Russian stress is — one strong syllable in every word","Found the stressed syllable in real words, by ear and by the mark","Heard how moving the stress turns castle [ZA-mok] into lock [za-MOK]","Read five everyday words aloud with the stress in the right place"],"review":{"intro":"Before the new idea, a quick look back at Lesson 7, where the whole alphabet came together. Three little checks — no pressure, just waking the memory up.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just a rep — it all counts.","q":"Quick alphabet check: which sound does the letter <b>Ж</b> make?","opts":["zh — like the s in “pleasure”","j — like in “jam”","g — like in “go”"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Ж</b> = <b>zh</b>, the buzzing sound in “plea<b>s</b>ure”. One of the brand-new shapes you tamed in Lesson 5."},{"type":"choice","q":"And a classic trap: the letter <b>С</b> always sounds like —","opts":["s (as in “sun”)","k (as in “cat”)","ch (as in “chat”)"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>С</b> is a false friend — it looks like English C but it is always <b>s</b>, never k. You caught it, which means the trap is losing its power."},{"type":"choice","q":"Cold read, just like at the end of Lesson 7: <b>спаси́бо</b> means —","opts":["thank you","please","goodbye"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>спаси́бо</b> = thank you. And by the end of today you'll know exactly which part of it to say louder."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · One loud syllable","title":"Every word has exactly <em>one</em> boss","paras":["Say the English word “banana” out loud. Hear how the middle part is louder — ba-NA-na? Russian does the very same thing, only turned up. Every Russian word has exactly <b>one</b> stressed syllable, and it is noticeably louder, longer and clearer than all the others — much stronger than English stress.","The little mark you've been seeing (о́, а́, и́…) shows you where that loud syllable lives. Listen to these three words and say them back, really leaning on the marked part: <b>окно́</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"окно\">🔊</button>, <b>соба́ка</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"собака\">🔊</button>, <b>хорошо́</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"хорошо\">🔊</button>. Exaggerate it — sounding a bit theatrical now is exactly how the habit forms."],"audio":"Say the English word banana out loud. Hear how the middle part is louder? Russian does the very same thing, only turned up. Every Russian word has exactly one stressed syllable, and it is noticeably louder, longer and clearer than all the others. The little mark shows you where that loud syllable lives. Listen to окно, собака, хорошо, and say them back, really leaning on the loud part.","examples":[{"ru":"window","en":"<b>окно́</b>","gloss":"[ok-NO]","say":"окно"},{"ru":"dog","en":"<b>соба́ка</b>","gloss":"[so-BA-ka]","say":"собака"},{"ru":"well / good","en":"<b>хорошо́</b>","gloss":"[kho-ro-SHO]","say":"хорошо"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> pick the syllable that carries the stress — the one you'd say loudest.","q":"Where's the stress in <b>соба́ка</b> (dog)?","opts":["со-","-ба́-","-ка"],"answer":1,"explain":"The mark sits on the middle <b>а́</b>: so-<b>BA</b>-ka. Punch that middle syllable and the word sounds Russian at once."},{"type":"choice","q":"And in <b>окно́</b> (window)?","opts":["ок-","-но́","both syllables equally"],"answer":1,"explain":"It's ok-<b>NO</b> — the end carries the weight. And “both equally” never happens in Russian: one word, one boss."},{"type":"speak","target":"соба́ка","sub":"[so-BA-ka] — make the middle syllable clearly the loudest","want":"собака"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The honest part","title":"Stress is unpredictable — <em>and invisible</em>","paras":["Here's the honest truth: there is no rule that tells you where Russian stress falls. It can land on any syllable — first, middle or last — and you learn each word's stress together with the word itself. English quietly does the same (compare PHO-to-graph and pho-TO-gra-phy), so your ear already knows this game.","And one more honest thing: real Russian text does <b>not</b> print the stress mark. Books, street signs, websites — no marks anywhere. We add them in this course as training wheels, and later, when your ear is stronger, the crutch comes off. I'll warn you before that happens — promise.","Why stress deserves a whole lesson: sometimes it is the <b>only</b> difference between two words. <b>За́мок</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"замок\">🔊</button> with the stress up front is a <b>castle</b>. Slide the stress to the end — <b>замо́к</b> — and it's a <b>lock</b> on a door. Same six letters, two different words."],"audio":"Here's the honest truth: there is no rule that tells you where Russian stress falls. You learn each word's stress together with the word itself. English quietly does the same: compare PHOtograph and phoTOgraphy. And one more honest thing: real Russian text does not print the stress mark. We add it as training wheels, and later the crutch comes off. Why it matters: замок with the stress up front is a castle. Slide the stress to the end, and it's a lock on a door. Same letters, two different words.","examples":[{"ru":"castle (stress at the start)","en":"<b>за́мок</b>","gloss":"[ZA-mok]","say":"замок"},{"ru":"lock (stress at the end)","en":"<b>замо́к</b>","gloss":"[za-MOK]","say":"замок"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You see <b>за́мок</b> — stress on the first syllable. What is it?","opts":["a castle","a lock","a key"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>За́мок</b> [ZA-mok] = castle. Slide the stress to the end — <b>замо́к</b> [za-MOK] — and you're suddenly talking about a lock. Stress is part of the word, not decoration."},{"type":"choice","q":"Will you see stress marks in a real Russian book?","opts":["No — this course adds them only to help you learn","Yes — they're part of Russian spelling","Only on capital letters"],"answer":0,"explain":"Native text hides the stress completely. Our marks are training wheels — there now, gone later, exactly when you're ready."},{"type":"speak","target":"замо́к","sub":"[za-MOK] — stress at the very end: it's a lock","want":"замок"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Find the beat","title":"Clap it, hum it — <em>own the rhythm</em>","paras":["Here's a trick that works better than staring at marks: treat every word as a tiny drum pattern. Hum it first, without the sounds — <b>спаси́бо</b> is da-DA-da, <b>хорошо́</b> is da-da-DA. Then say the word and clap once, exactly on the loud syllable. Your mouth learns rhythm faster than your eyes ever will.","Try it now on the longest word of the day: <b>пожа́луйста</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пожалуйста\">🔊</button> (please). Four syllables, and the second one is the boss: po-<b>ZHA</b>-lu-sta. Hum it, clap it, say it. This word looks like a monster today — and in a week it will roll off your tongue without a thought."],"audio":"Here's a trick that works better than staring at marks: treat every word as a tiny drum pattern. Hum it first, without the sounds. Спасибо is da-DA-da. Хорошо is da-da-DA. Then say the word and clap once, exactly on the loud syllable. Try it on the longest word of the day: пожалуйста. Four syllables, and the second one is the boss. Hum it, clap it, say it.","examples":[{"ru":"thank you","en":"<b>спаси́бо</b>","gloss":"[spa-SEE-bo] — da-DA-da","say":"спасибо"},{"ru":"please / you're welcome","en":"<b>пожа́луйста</b>","gloss":"[po-ZHA-lu-sta] — da-DA-da-da","say":"пожалуйста"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> hum the word in your head first, then pick its drum pattern (DA = the loud syllable).","q":"Which pattern matches <b>хорошо́</b> (well)?","opts":["DA-da-da","da-DA-da","da-da-DA"],"answer":2,"explain":"The mark sits on the final <b>о́</b>: ho-ro-<b>SHO</b> — da-da-DA. The end gets the punch."},{"type":"choice","q":"Where's the stress in <b>пожа́луйста</b> (please)?","opts":["по-","-жа́-","-ста"],"answer":1,"explain":"po-<b>ZHA</b>-lu-sta — the second syllable is the boss. Clap on ZHA and the rest of the word falls into place around it."},{"type":"speak","target":"спаси́бо","sub":"[spa-SEE-bo] — clap on SEE as you say it","want":"спасибо"},{"type":"speak","target":"пожа́луйста","sub":"[po-ZHA-lu-sta] — the second syllable is the loud one","want":"пожалуйста"}]}],"vocab":{"intro":"Today's words: five you'll use every single day, plus the famous castle-and-lock pair. Flip each card, then say the word out loud with the stress landing hard on the marked syllable.","items":[{"en":"окно́","ru":"window","ex":"[ok-NO] — a window"},{"en":"соба́ка","ru":"dog","ex":"[so-BA-ka] — a dog"},{"en":"хорошо́","ru":"well, good, OK","ex":"[kho-ro-SHO] — well / OK"},{"en":"спаси́бо","ru":"thank you","ex":"[spa-SEE-bo] — thanks"},{"en":"пожа́луйста","ru":"please; you're welcome","ex":"[po-ZHA-lu-sta] — please"},{"en":"за́мок","ru":"castle","ex":"[ZA-mok] — stress up front: a castle"},{"en":"замо́к","ru":"lock","ex":"[za-MOK] — stress at the end: a lock"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each word to its loud syllable — the one that carries the stress. Hum the word first if you're not sure.","pairs":[["окно́","-но́"],["соба́ка","-ба́-"],["хорошо́","-шо́"],["спаси́бо","-си́-"],["пожа́луйста","-жа́-"],["за́мок","за́-"]]},"reading":{"intro":"Read each word aloud — slowly first, then at normal speed. Tap 🔊 to compare with the recording. Make the marked syllable clearly the loudest; exaggerating it a little is exactly right at this stage.","title":"Чита́ем с ударе́нием! (Reading with stress)","sentences":["окно́","соба́ка","хорошо́","спаси́бо","пожа́луйста","за́мок","замо́к"],"translation":"window · dog · well · thank you · please · castle · lock"},"quiz":{"intro":"Last stretch — five quick checks on today's big idea. Take your time; you've got this. 💛","items":[{"q":"1. How many stressed syllables does a Russian word have?","opts":["Exactly one","One at the start and one at the end","None — all syllables sound the same"],"answer":0,"explain":"One word, one boss. Every Russian word has exactly <b>one</b> loud syllable — and it's much stronger than English stress."},{"q":"2. Where's the stress in <b>окно́</b> (window)?","opts":["ок-","-но́","both syllables equally"],"answer":1,"explain":"ok-<b>NO</b> — the mark on о́ shows the end is the loud part. “Both equally” never happens in Russian."},{"q":"3. <b>За́мок</b> [ZA-mok] and <b>замо́к</b> [za-MOK] are —","opts":["two different words: castle and lock","the same word, said politely and casually","singular and plural of one word"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only the stress differs, and it flips the meaning: <b>за́мок</b> = castle, <b>замо́к</b> = lock. That's why stress got its own lesson."},{"q":"4. Do real Russian books and street signs print the stress mark?","opts":["No — this course adds it as a learning aid","Yes — it's a required part of spelling","Only on the first word of a sentence"],"answer":0,"explain":"Native text hides the stress; our marks are training wheels that come off later. (Fun fact: dictionaries and children's primers are about the only places that do print it.)"},{"q":"5. Hum it: which drum pattern matches <b>спаси́бо</b> (thank you)?","opts":["DA-da-da","da-DA-da","da-da-DA"],"answer":1,"explain":"spa-<b>SEE</b>-bo — da-DA-da. Clap on the middle syllable and you've got it."}]}},
"9": {"day":9,"week":"02","themeRu":"Vowel reduction","themeEn":"А́канье: о → [a]","intro":"Today you get the most liberating rule in Russian pronunciation. Maybe you've noticed that the word for “good” is spelled with three o's, yet Russians say [kha-ra-SHO] — only one real [o] survives. That's not sloppiness; it's the rule. And it's wonderful news: once you find the loud syllable, every other vowel relaxes on its own. Less work, not more. Let me show you with a glass of milk. 🥛","introAudio":"Today you get the most liberating rule in Russian pronunciation. Maybe you've noticed that the word for good is spelled with three o's, yet Russians say kha-ra-SHO. Only one real o survives. That's not sloppiness, it's the rule. And it's wonderful news: once you find the loud syllable, every other vowel relaxes on its own. Less work, not more. Let me show you with a glass of milk.","goals":["Discover “akanye” — the rule that an unstressed <b>o</b> relaxes into [a]","Read the Russian word for milk the way Russians say it: [ma-la-KO]","Let vowels far from the stress fade into a soft, lazy sound","Read real words — Moscow, water, weather — with natural, relaxed vowels"],"learned":["Learned “akanye” — an unstressed <b>o</b> relaxes into [a]","Read [ma-la-KO] out of a spelling with three o's","Let the vowels far from the stress go soft and lazy","Read Moscow, water, weather and more with natural vowels"],"review":{"intro":"Before the new rule, a quick look back at Lesson 8 — stress. Today's whole lesson hangs on it, so let's make sure it's warm in your memory.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Lesson 8 flashback: where is the stress in <b>спаси́бо</b> (thank you)?","opts":["the middle: spa-SEE-ba","the first syllable: SPA-see-ba","the last: spa-see-BA"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>спаси́бо</b> is loud in the middle: [spa-SEE-ba]. And notice — the final о already comes out as [a]. That's exactly today's topic."},{"type":"choice","q":"Where is the stress in <b>окно́</b> (window)?","opts":["the first syllable: OK-no","the last syllable: ak-NO","both syllables equally"],"answer":1,"explain":"The mark sits on the о́ at the end: [ak-NO]. One loud syllable per word — never two."},{"type":"choice","q":"How many stressed syllables does one Russian word have?","opts":["Exactly one","One for every vowel","It depends on the speaker"],"answer":0,"explain":"Exactly one loud syllable per word. Today you'll see why that single syllable decides how all the others sound."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Akanye","title":"Unstressed <em>о</em> turns into [a]","paras":["Here is the rule Russians live by: the letter <b>о</b> makes a real [o] sound <b>only when it's stressed</b>. Everywhere else it relaxes into [a]. This habit even has a name — <b>а́канье</b> (“a-kanye”), literally “a-ing”.","Watch it work in <b>молоко́</b> (milk). The stress mark sits on the last syllable, so that's the only true [o]: <b>мо</b> → [ma], <b>ло</b> → [la], <b>ко́</b> → [KO]. Three о's on paper — one [o] out loud: <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"молоко\">🔊</button> [ma-la-KO]."],"audio":"Here is the rule Russians live by: the letter о makes a real o sound only when it's stressed. Everywhere else it relaxes into a. This habit even has a name: аканье. Watch it work in молоко, milk. The stress sits on the last syllable, so that's the only true o. ма, ла, ко. Three о letters on paper, one o out loud: молоко.","table":{"rows":[["<b>мо</b>","unstressed → relaxed <b>[ma]</b>"],["<b>ло</b>","unstressed → relaxed <b>[la]</b>"],["<b>ко́</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ко\">🔊</button>","stressed → a clear, round <b>[KO]</b>"]]},"examples":[{"ru":"milk","en":"<b>молоко́</b>","gloss":"[ma-la-KO]","say":"молоко"},{"ru":"water","en":"<b>вода́</b>","gloss":"[va-DA]","say":"вода"},{"ru":"Moscow","en":"<b>Москва́</b>","gloss":"[mask-VA]","say":"Москва"},{"ru":"good / well","en":"<b>хорошо́</b>","gloss":"[kha-ra-SHO]","say":"хорошо"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is a working moment, not a mistake.","q":"How does the <b>first о</b> in <b>молоко́</b> sound?","opts":["[o] — clear and round","[a] — relaxed, because it's unstressed","it disappears completely"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only the stressed syllable gets the real [o]. The first two о's relax into [a]: [ma-la-KO]."},{"type":"choice","q":"In <b>вода́</b> (water) the stress is on the last syllable. So the whole word sounds like —","opts":["[VO-da]","[va-DA]","[vo-DO]"],"answer":1,"explain":"The а́ at the end is the loud part, so the unstressed о relaxes: <b>вода́</b> = [va-DA]."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The lazy vowel","title":"Further from the stress — softer still","paras":["One small refinement, and I promise not to drown you in phonetics. The further a vowel sits from the stress, the lazier it gets. In <b>хорошо́</b> the first syllable is a quick, blurry little sound — like the “a” at the end of English <i>sofa</i>. No chart needed. Just think: <b>the loud syllable gets full colour; everything else is relaxed and a bit grey</b>.","This is why Lesson 8 mattered so much. Stress isn't decoration — it's the key that tells you how the whole word sounds. Find the loud syllable, give it your energy, and let the rest go soft. Russian pronunciation rewards relaxing, not trying harder."],"audio":"One small refinement, and I promise not to drown you in phonetics. The further a vowel sits from the stress, the lazier it gets. In хорошо the first syllable is a quick, blurry little sound, like the a at the end of the English word sofa. No chart needed. The loud syllable gets full colour, everything else is relaxed and a bit grey. This is why stress matters so much. Find the loud syllable, give it your energy, and let the rest go soft.","examples":[{"ru":"weather","en":"<b>пого́да</b>","gloss":"[pa-GO-da]","say":"погода"},{"ru":"window","en":"<b>окно́</b>","gloss":"[ak-NO]","say":"окно"},{"ru":"she","en":"<b>она́</b>","gloss":"[a-NA]","say":"она"},{"ru":"please","en":"<b>пожа́луйста</b>","gloss":"[pa-ZHA-lu-sta] — the о relaxes and the й all but disappears","say":"пожалуйста"}],"tasks":[{"type":"drag","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each spelled word to the way it actually sounds. Whisper the sound version as you drag.","q":"Match the spelling to the sound:","pairs":[["окно́","[ak-NO]"],["она́","[a-NA]"],["пого́да","[pa-GO-da]"]]},{"type":"speak","target":"молоко́","sub":"[ma-la-KO] — save the one real [o] for the end","want":"молоко"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · е and я join in","title":"Unstressed <em>е</em> and <em>я</em> lean towards [i]","paras":["The relaxing club has two more members. When <b>е</b> and <b>я</b> are unstressed, they slide towards a light [i]. The word <b>язы́к</b> (language) is spelled ya-zyk, but what you hear is [yi-ZYK]. Same story in <b>метро́</b> — spelled me-tro, sounds like [mi-TRO].","Just notice it for now — no need to force anything. Listen, copy what you hear, and your mouth will pick up the habit on its own."],"audio":"The relaxing club has two more members. When е and я are unstressed, they slide towards a light i sound. The word язык, language, is spelled ya-zyk, but what you hear is yi-ZYK. Same story in метро: spelled me-tro, sounds like mi-TRO. Just notice it for now. Listen, copy what you hear, and your mouth will pick up the habit on its own.","examples":[{"ru":"language / tongue","en":"<b>язы́к</b>","gloss":"[yi-ZYK]","say":"язык"},{"ru":"metro, subway","en":"<b>метро́</b>","gloss":"[mi-TRO]","say":"метро"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The <b>я</b> in <b>язы́к</b> is unstressed. What do you actually hear?","opts":["a light [yi]","a loud, full [YA]","a round [o]"],"answer":0,"explain":"Unstressed <b>я</b> relaxes towards [i]: [yi-ZYK]. The full [ya] only shows up under stress."},{"type":"speak","target":"Москва́","sub":"[mask-VA] — the о relaxes into [a]","want":"Москва"}]}],"vocab":{"intro":"Today's words — every one of them hides a relaxed vowel. Flip a card, find the stressed syllable first, and let the rest go soft.","items":[{"en":"молоко́","ru":"milk","ex":"[ma-la-KO] — only the last о is a real [o]"},{"en":"Москва́","ru":"Moscow","ex":"[mask-VA] — the capital of Russia"},{"en":"хорошо́","ru":"good, well","ex":"[kha-ra-SHO] — three о's, one [o]"},{"en":"вода́","ru":"water","ex":"[va-DA] — water"},{"en":"пого́да","ru":"weather","ex":"[pa-GO-da] — the weather"},{"en":"окно́","ru":"window","ex":"[ak-NO] — a window"},{"en":"она́","ru":"she","ex":"[a-NA] — she"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each spelling to the way it really sounds. Say the sound version softly as you drag — that's the whole trick.","pairs":[["молоко́","[ma-la-KO]"],["Москва́","[mask-VA]"],["хорошо́","[kha-ra-SHO]"],["вода́","[va-DA]"],["пого́да","[pa-GO-da]"],["язы́к","[yi-ZYK]"]]},"reading":{"intro":"Read each word aloud. Find the stress mark first — that syllable gets your energy; every other vowel relaxes on its own. Tap 🔊 to compare with the voice.","title":"Чита́ем! (Let's read)","sentences":["молоко́","Москва́","хорошо́","вода́","пого́да","окно́","она́"],"translation":"milk · Moscow · good/well · water · weather · window · she"},"quiz":{"intro":"Last stretch — five quick checks and the lazy-vowel rule is yours. 💛","items":[{"q":"1. The first <b>о</b> in <b>молоко́</b> sounds like —","opts":["[o], clear and round","[a], relaxed","[u]"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only the stressed о keeps its [o]. The others relax: [ma-la-KO]."},{"q":"2. When does the letter <b>о</b> make a true [o] sound?","opts":["Always — it's written о","Only when it's stressed","Only at the start of a word"],"answer":1,"explain":"о = [o] only under stress. Unstressed, it relaxes into [a] — that's akanye."},{"q":"3. Which is the right way to say <b>вода́</b> (water)?","opts":["[VO-da]","[vo-DA]","[va-DA]"],"answer":2,"explain":"The stress is on the end, so the о relaxes into [a]: [va-DA]."},{"q":"4. Unstressed <b>е</b> and <b>я</b> lean towards —","opts":["a light [i]","a loud [A]","a round [O]"],"answer":0,"explain":"They slide to [i]: <b>язы́к</b> = [yi-ZYK], <b>метро́</b> = [mi-TRO]."},{"q":"5. Why does finding the stress unlock the whole word?","opts":["It changes the spelling","It decides how all the other vowels sound","It makes the consonants softer"],"answer":1,"explain":"One loud syllable — and every other vowel relaxes around it. Know the stress, and the word almost reads itself."}]}},
"10": {"day":10,"week":"02","themeRu":"Hard & soft","themeEn":"Твёрдо и мя́гко","intro":"Today is the big one — the single biggest idea in all of Russian reading. Almost every consonant comes in two flavours: <b>hard</b> (the plain sound you know) and <b>soft</b> (the same sound with a tiny hidden \"y\" pressed inside it). Whole pairs of real words differ by nothing else — and the mysterious silent sign from Lesson 6 finally gets its secret explained today. Your only job is to <b>hear</b> the difference and know which letters cause it; saying it perfectly comes later, and that's completely fine. Let's listen closely. 💛","introAudio":"Today is the big one — the single biggest idea in all of Russian reading. Almost every consonant comes in two flavours: hard, the plain sound you know, and soft, the same sound with a tiny hidden y pressed inside it. Whole pairs of real words differ by nothing else — and the mysterious silent sign from Lesson 6 finally gets its secret explained today. Your only job is to hear the difference and know which letters cause it. Saying it perfectly comes later, and that's completely fine. Let's listen closely.","goals":["Discover that every Russian consonant has two flavours — <b>hard</b> and <b>soft</b>","Learn the six softeners: <b>Ь</b> and the vowels <b>Я Е Ё Ю И</b>","Finally understand what the soft sign <b>Ь</b> really does","Hear the difference in real word pairs like <b>ма́т</b> / <b>ма́ть</b>"],"learned":["Discovered the two flavours of every Russian consonant — hard and soft","Learned which letters soften a consonant: <b>Ь</b> and <b>Я Е Ё Ю И</b>","Uncovered the real job of the soft sign <b>Ь</b> — the promise from Lesson 6, kept","Heard and read real minimal pairs: <b>ма́т</b>/<b>ма́ть</b>, <b>но́с</b>/<b>нёс</b>, <b>лу́к</b>/<b>лю́к</b>"],"review":{"intro":"First, a quick look back. Yesterday you learned that unstressed letters don't always say their name. Let's check that it still lives in your head — you'll need sharp ears today.","introAudio":"First, a quick look back. Yesterday you learned that unstressed letters don't always say their name. Let's check that it still lives in your head — you'll need sharp ears today.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just your memory warming up.","q":"Yesterday's rule: how do the first two <b>о</b>'s sound in <b>молоко́</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"молоко\">🔊</button>?","opts":["like a clear, full [o] — all three are the same","they are silent","like a quick [a] — only the stressed о́ is a true [o]"],"answer":2,"explain":"That's akanye: an unstressed <b>о</b> relaxes into a quick [a]. Only the stressed о́ keeps its full sound — [ma-la-KO]."},{"type":"choice","q":"And in <b>вода́</b> (water) — how does the first <b>о</b> sound?","opts":["[a] — it's unstressed, so it relaxes","[o] — о always sounds like o","[u] — it becomes a у"],"answer":0,"explain":"Same rule at work: the stress sits on <b>а́</b>, so the unstressed о turns into [a] — [va-DA]."},{"type":"speak","target":"молоко́","sub":"[ma-la-KO] — two quick a's, then the stressed o","want":"молоко"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Two flavours","title":"Every consonant has <em>two flavours</em>","paras":["Here is the biggest secret of Russian reading: almost every consonant exists in two versions. The <b>hard</b> one is the plain sound you already know. The <b>soft</b> one is the same sound with a tiny hidden \"y\" pressed into it — the middle of your tongue rises toward the roof of your mouth, like the very start of English \"yes\". English doesn't use this switch, so at first your ear may refuse to hear it. That's completely normal — give it a few listens.","Now hear it in a real pair. <b>ма́т</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мат\">🔊</button> [mat] means \"checkmate\" — hard т. <b>ма́ть</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мать\">🔊</button> [mat'] means \"mother\" — soft т. One tiny flavour, a completely different word. And there it is — the soft sign <b>Ь</b> from Lesson 6, finally doing its job in the open: it has no sound of its own, it simply makes the consonant before it soft!"],"audio":"Here is the biggest secret of Russian reading: almost every consonant exists in two versions. The hard one is the plain sound you already know. The soft one is the same sound with a tiny hidden y pressed into it, like the very start of the English word yes. Now hear it in a real pair. мат, with a hard t, means checkmate. мать, with a soft t, means mother. One tiny flavour, a completely different word. And that is what the soft sign really does: it has no sound of its own, it simply makes the consonant before it soft.","examples":[{"ru":"checkmate","en":"<b>ма́т</b>","gloss":"[mat] — hard т","say":"мат"},{"ru":"mother","en":"<b>ма́ть</b>","gloss":"[mat'] — soft т","say":"мать"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> listen to both words first, then tap the answer.","q":"<b>ма́т</b> and <b>ма́ть</b> differ by one letter — the soft sign <b>Ь</b>. What does it do?","opts":["It adds an extra syllable at the end","It makes the т before it soft — a tiny \"y\" flavour","It makes the а sound longer"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Ь</b> is silent — no syllable, no vowel change. Its whole job is to soften the consonant before it: [mat] → [mat']."},{"type":"speak","target":"ма́ть","sub":"[mat'] — end with a soft т, like a whisper of \"y\" tucked into it","want":"мать"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The softeners","title":"Who makes a consonant soft? <em>Ь and Я Е Ё Ю И</em>","paras":["A consonant goes <b>soft</b> in two situations: before the soft sign <b>Ь</b>, and before one of the five vowels <b>Я Е Ё Ю И</b>. Everywhere else it stays <b>hard</b> — including before <b>Ы Э О А У</b> and at the end of a word (unless Ь is there). That's the whole rule: five vowels plus one silent sign, and you can tell hard from soft anywhere.","This also solves a small mystery from Lesson 6. After a consonant, <b>я е ё ю</b> don't usually add a full \"y\" sound — they spend their \"y\" on softening the consonant. So <b>ня</b> is a soft н + [a], not \"n-ya\". Compare the columns below — tap every 🔊 and listen for the flavour, not the spelling.","Three letters ignore the softeners: <b>ж</b>, <b>ш</b> and <b>ц</b> stay hard no matter what comes after them — remember жи/ши from Lesson 5 — and <b>ч</b>, <b>щ</b> are always soft, with or without helpers. The final lesson comes back to them."],"audio":"A consonant goes soft in exactly two situations: before the soft sign, and before one of the five vowels я, е, ё, ю, и. Everywhere else it stays hard, including before ы, э, о, а, у, and at the end of a word unless the soft sign is there. After a consonant, the vowels я, е, ё, ю spend their y on softening that consonant. Compare the columns and listen for the flavour, not the spelling. Three letters ignore the softeners: ж, ш and ц stay hard no matter what comes after them, and ч, щ are always soft. The final lesson comes back to them.","table":{"rows":[["<b>hard</b> — before Ы Э О А У (or at the end)","<b>soft</b> — before Ь and Я Е Ё Ю И"],["та <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"та\">🔊</button> [ta]","тя <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тя\">🔊</button> [t'a]"],["ну <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ну\">🔊</button> [nu]","ню <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ню\">🔊</button> [n'u]"],["но <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"но\">🔊</button> [no]","нё <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"нё\">🔊</button> [n'o]"],["мы <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мы\">🔊</button> [my]","ми <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ми\">🔊</button> [m'ee]"],["лу <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"лу\">🔊</button> [lu]","лю <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"лю\">🔊</button> [l'u]"]]},"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which letters make the consonant before them <b>soft</b>?","opts":["Ы Э О А У","Ъ and Й","Ь and Я Е Ё Ю И"],"answer":2,"explain":"The six softeners: the silent <b>Ь</b> plus the vowels <b>Я Е Ё Ю И</b>. Before Ы Э О А У — or at the end of a word with no Ь — a consonant stays hard."},{"type":"choice","q":"In <b>лю́к</b> (hatch), is the <b>л</b> hard or soft?","opts":["hard — л is always hard","soft — ю softens it","silent — л drops out before ю"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ю</b> is one of the five softening vowels, so the л in <b>лю́к</b> is soft: [l'ook]. In <b>лу́к</b> (onion) the л is hard: [look]."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Train your ear","title":"Minimal pairs — <em>hear the flavour</em>","paras":["Now the training ground: pairs of real words where the only difference is hard vs soft. Play each pair a few times. First just notice that they're different; then try to catch which one is the soft one. Your ear learns this before your mouth does — that's the natural order.","A gentle promise: nobody expects you to pronounce the soft ones perfectly today. Russians will understand you anyway. Your goal for now is to hear the difference and to know which letters caused it — that skill alone lets you read every one of these words correctly."],"audio":"Now the training ground: pairs of real words where the only difference is hard versus soft. Play each pair a few times. First just notice that they are different, then try to catch which one is the soft one. Your ear learns this before your mouth does. Nobody expects you to pronounce the soft ones perfectly today. Your goal is to hear the difference and to know which letters caused it.","examples":[{"ru":"checkmate → mother","en":"<b>ма́т</b> → <b>ма́ть</b>","gloss":"[mat] → [mat']","say":"мат, мать"},{"ru":"brother → to take","en":"<b>бра́т</b> → <b>бра́ть</b>","gloss":"[brat] → [brat']","say":"брат, брать"},{"ru":"nose → (he) carried","en":"<b>но́с</b> → <b>нёс</b>","gloss":"[nos] → [n'os]","say":"нос, нёс"},{"ru":"onion → hatch","en":"<b>лу́к</b> → <b>лю́к</b>","gloss":"[look] → [l'ook]","say":"лук, люк"},{"ru":"(he) washed → (he is) sweet, dear","en":"<b>мы́л</b> → <b>ми́л</b>","gloss":"[myl] → [m'eel]","say":"мыл, мил"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"One of these means \"to take\". Which one?","opts":["бра́ть — soft т (with Ь)","бра́т — hard т"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>бра́т</b> [brat] is \"brother\" — you've known it since Lesson 3. Add the soft sign and the т goes soft: <b>бра́ть</b> [brat'] — \"to take\". One flavour, a whole new word."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> say both words of the pair, slowly — hard one first.","target":"но́с — нёс","sub":"[nos] — [n'os]: hard н, then soft н","want":"нос нёс"},{"type":"speak","target":"лу́к — лю́к","sub":"[look] — [l'ook]: hard л, then soft л","want":"лук люк"}]}],"vocab":{"intro":"Today's words come in pairs — hard vs soft. Flip each card, listen, and find its partner in your head.","items":[{"en":"ма́т","ru":"checkmate","ex":"[mat] — hard т at the end"},{"en":"ма́ть","ru":"mother","ex":"[mat'] — soft т, thanks to Ь"},{"en":"бра́т","ru":"brother","ex":"[brat] — hard т; a friend from Lesson 3"},{"en":"бра́ть","ru":"to take","ex":"[brat'] — soft т makes a new word"},{"en":"но́с","ru":"nose","ex":"[nos] — hard н"},{"en":"нёс","ru":"(he) carried","ex":"[n'os] — soft н, thanks to ё"},{"en":"лу́к","ru":"onion","ex":"[look] — hard л"},{"en":"лю́к","ru":"hatch, manhole","ex":"[l'ook] — soft л, thanks to ю"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each word to its meaning. The soft consonants are what tell the words apart — trust your ear.","pairs":[["ма́ть","mother"],["ма́т","checkmate"],["бра́ть","to take"],["но́с","nose"],["нёс","carried"],["лу́к","onion"],["лю́к","hatch"]]},"reading":{"intro":"Read each pair aloud, slowly — hard word first, soft word second. Tap 🔊 to hear it before you try. Feel the tiny \"y\" appear in the second word.","title":"Чита́ем па́ры! (Let's read the pairs)","sentences":["ма́т — ма́ть","бра́т — бра́ть","но́с — нёс","лу́к — лю́к","мы́л — ми́л"],"translation":"checkmate — mother · brother — to take · nose — carried · onion — hatch · washed — sweet"},"quiz":{"intro":"Last stretch of the biggest lesson in the course. Five quick checks — you've earned this. 💛","items":[{"q":"1. A consonant goes <b>soft</b> before —","opts":["Ы Э О А У","only a double consonant","Ь and Я Е Ё Ю И"],"answer":2,"explain":"The six softeners: the silent <b>Ь</b> plus the vowels <b>Я Е Ё Ю И</b>. Before Ы Э О А У a consonant stays hard. (Three rebels ignore the softeners: ж, ш, ц stay hard, and ч, щ are always soft — Lesson 12 returns to them.)"},{"q":"2. What does the soft sign <b>Ь</b> do?","opts":["It makes the consonant before it soft — it has no sound of its own","It has its own quiet \"soft\" sound","It marks the stressed syllable"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lesson 6 promised you'd find out — now you know. <b>Ь</b> is silent, and its whole job is to soften the consonant before it: ма́т [mat] → ма́ть [mat']."},{"q":"3. Read it: <b>бра́ть</b> means —","opts":["brother","to take","carried"],"answer":1,"explain":"Soft т = <b>бра́ть</b>, \"to take\". Hard т = <b>бра́т</b>, \"brother\". And \"carried\" is <b>нёс</b> — a different pair entirely."},{"q":"4. In <b>нёс</b> (carried), the <b>н</b> is —","opts":["hard","silent","soft — ё softens it"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>ё</b> is one of the five softening vowels, so the н is soft: [n'os]. In <b>но́с</b> (nose) the н is hard: [nos]."},{"q":"5. Which word means \"onion\"?","opts":["лю́к","нёс","лу́к"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>лу́к</b> [look], hard л — the onion. <b>лю́к</b> [l'ook], soft л — a hatch in the street. One tiny flavour keeps your soup safe."}]}},
"11": {"day":11,"week":"02","themeRu":"Voicing & devoicing","themeEn":"Хлеб → [хлеп]","intro":"Today I'm letting you in on something Russians do without even noticing: at the end of a word, they switch their voice off a little early. The word for bread is spelled with a loud <b>b</b> at the end — but it comes out as a quiet <b>p</b>. Your eye reads the spelling, your mouth says the lazy version. That's not sloppiness, that's the rule! Once you hear it, you'll spot it everywhere — and you'll sound more natural right away. 💛","introAudio":"Today I'm letting you in on something Russians do without even noticing: at the end of a word, they switch their voice off a little early. The word for bread is spelled with a loud b at the end, but it comes out as a quiet p. Your eye reads the spelling, your mouth says the lazy version. That's not sloppiness, that's the rule. Once you hear it, you'll spot it everywhere, and you'll sound more natural right away.","goals":["Meet the six “twin” consonant pairs — loud <b>b, v, g, d, zh, z</b> and their quiet partners <b>p, f, k, t, sh, s</b>","Hear a loud consonant go quiet at the very end of a word — bread is spelled with a [b] but ends in [p]","Spot the same trick inside words, when a loud twin stands right before a quiet one","Read real words the lazy, natural way: bread, friend, knife, city, everything"],"learned":["Met the six loud/quiet twin pairs — b/p, v/f, g/k, d/t, zh/sh, z/s","Heard loud consonants go quiet at the end of a word — [khlep], [drook], [nosh]","Caught devoicing inside words too — [VOT-ka], [fsyo]","Read a word where two rules team up: reduction plus devoicing in [GO-rat]"],"review":{"intro":"Before the new trick, a quick look back at last lesson — soft and hard consonants. Thirty seconds of memory work makes today stick better.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just your memory warming up.","q":"Which letters make the consonant <b>before</b> them soft?","opts":["А, О, У, Ы, Э","Я, Е, Ё, Ю, И — and the soft sign Ь","Ж, Ш, Ц"],"answer":1,"explain":"The five softeners <b>Я Е Ё Ю И</b> plus <b>Ь</b> melt the consonant in front of them. А О У Ы Э keep it hard."},{"type":"choice","q":"<b>ма́т</b> vs <b>ма́ть</b> — what does the <b>Ь</b> at the end of <b>ма́ть</b> do?","opts":["It softens the т — it has no sound of its own","It adds an extra [i] sound","It marks the stress"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Ь</b> is silent — its whole job is to soften the consonant before it. That's why мат and ма́ть sound different."},{"type":"choice","q":"In <b>нёс</b> (carried), the <b>н</b> is —","opts":["hard — ё changes nothing","silent","soft — the ё softens it"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>ё</b> is one of the five softeners, so the <b>н</b> in нёс goes soft. Compare it with the hard н in но́с (nose)."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Loud twin, quiet twin","title":"Six sounds come <em>in pairs</em>","paras":["Six Russian consonants come in pairs: a <b>loud twin</b> and a <b>quiet twin</b>. Your mouth does exactly the same thing for both — the only difference is whether your voice is buzzing. Try it: put your fingers on your throat and say a long <b>zzz</b>, then a long <b>sss</b>. Feel the buzz switch off? That's the entire difference between <b>з</b> and <b>с</b>.","Here are all six pairs. Tap each 🔊, keep your fingers on your throat, and feel the voice turn on and off. Same mouth, different switch."],"audio":"Six Russian consonants come in pairs: a loud twin and a quiet twin. Your mouth does exactly the same thing for both. The only difference is whether your voice is buzzing. Put your fingers on your throat and say a long zzz, then a long sss. Feel the buzz switch off? That's the entire difference. Tap each speaker, keep your fingers on your throat, and feel the voice turn on and off.","table":{"rows":[["<b>Б</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ба\">🔊</button> ↔ <b>П</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"па\">🔊</button>","loud <b>b</b> / quiet <b>p</b> — same lips"],["<b>В</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ва\">🔊</button> ↔ <b>Ф</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"фа\">🔊</button>","loud <b>v</b> / quiet <b>f</b> — same teeth on lip"],["<b>Г</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"га\">🔊</button> ↔ <b>К</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ка\">🔊</button>","loud <b>g</b> / quiet <b>k</b> — same back of the tongue"],["<b>Д</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"да\">🔊</button> ↔ <b>Т</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"та\">🔊</button>","loud <b>d</b> / quiet <b>t</b> — same tongue tap"],["<b>Ж</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"жа\">🔊</button> ↔ <b>Ш</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ша\">🔊</button>","loud <b>zh</b> / quiet <b>sh</b> — same hush shape"],["<b>З</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"за\">🔊</button> ↔ <b>С</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"са\">🔊</button>","loud <b>z</b> / quiet <b>s</b> — the throat-test pair"]]},"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the quiet twin. Think “same mouth, voice off”.","q":"Which letter is the quiet twin of <b>Д</b>?","opts":["П","К","Т"],"answer":2,"explain":"Same tongue tap, voice off: <b>д → т</b>. (П is the partner of Б, and К is the partner of Г.)"},{"type":"choice","q":"<b>Ж</b> [zh] switches its voice off. What does it turn into?","opts":["ш [sh]","с [s]","з [z]"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>ж</b> and <b>ш</b> make the same hushing shape — one buzzes, one doesn't. Voice off: <b>ж → ш</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Lazy endings","title":"At the end of a word, the voice <em>switches off</em>","paras":["Here's the rule itself: when a <b>loud twin</b> lands at the very end of a word, Russians switch the voice off early — it comes out as its <b>quiet partner</b>. The spelling never changes; only the sound relaxes. So <b>хлеб</b> (bread) is written with <b>б</b> but ends in [p]: <b>[khlep]</b>.","Walk through two more with me. <b>друг</b> (friend) ends in <b>г</b>, but you say <b>[drook]</b>. <b>нож</b> (knife) ends in <b>ж</b>, but you say <b>[nosh]</b>. Your eye reads the spelling, your mouth says the lazy version — Russians do this automatically, and from today you'll recognise it every time. You're not cutting corners; you're reading like a native."],"audio":"Here's the rule itself: when a loud twin lands at the very end of a word, Russians switch the voice off early. It comes out as its quiet partner. The spelling never changes; only the sound relaxes. So хлеб, bread, is written with б but ends in p: khlep. Walk through two more with me. друг, friend, ends in г, but you say drook. нож, knife, ends in ж, but you say nosh. Your eye reads the spelling, your mouth says the lazy version. Russians do this automatically, and from today you'll recognise it every time.","examples":[{"ru":"bread","en":"<b>хле́б</b>","gloss":"[khlep] — the б goes quiet: p","say":"хлеб"},{"ru":"friend","en":"<b>дру́г</b>","gloss":"[drook] — the г goes quiet: k","say":"друг"},{"ru":"knife","en":"<b>но́ж</b>","gloss":"[nosh] — the ж goes quiet: sh","say":"нож"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The final <b>б</b> in <b>хлеб</b> sounds like —","opts":["b","p","v"],"answer":1,"explain":"End of the word — voice off. The loud <b>б</b> relaxes into its quiet twin <b>п</b>: [khlep]."},{"type":"speak","target":"дру́г","sub":"[drook] — end it with a quiet k, not a g","want":"друг"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Team-ups","title":"Devoicing <em>inside</em> a word — and a double play","paras":["The ends of words aren't the only lazy spot. Inside a word, a loud twin standing right <b>before a quiet consonant</b> goes quiet too, so the pair matches. <b>во́дка</b> is said <b>[VOT-ka]</b> — the д quiets down to agree with the к. And <b>всё</b> (everything) starts with <b>вс</b>, so the в quiets to [f]: <b>[fsyo]</b>.","Now the star of the lesson: <b>го́род</b> (city). Two rules you already know team up in one word. The unstressed <b>о</b> reduces to [a] — that's Lesson 9 — and the final <b>д</b> devoices to [t] — that's today. Put them together: <b>[GO-rat]</b>. And <b>зову́т</b> (they call — as in “Меня́ зову́т…”, my name is…) needs no trick at all: the т at the end is already quiet. When you read го́род correctly, you're stacking rules like a native reader — that's a real milestone!"],"audio":"The ends of words aren't the only lazy spot. Inside a word, a loud twin standing right before a quiet consonant goes quiet too, so the pair matches. водка is said VOT-ka. The д quiets down to agree with the к. And всё, everything, starts with вс, so the в quiets to f: fsyo. Now the star of the lesson: город, city. Two rules you already know team up in one word. The unstressed о reduces to a, that's lesson nine, and the final д devoices to t, that's today. Put them together: GO-rat. And зовут, they call, needs no trick at all: the т at the end is already quiet. When you read город correctly, you're stacking rules like a native reader.","examples":[{"ru":"vodka","en":"<b>во́дка</b>","gloss":"[VOT-ka] — д quiets before к","say":"водка"},{"ru":"everything, all","en":"<b>всё</b>","gloss":"[fsyo] — в quiets before с","say":"всё"},{"ru":"city","en":"<b>го́род</b>","gloss":"[GO-rat] — reduction + devoicing together","say":"город"},{"ru":"they call (used to say your name)","en":"<b>зову́т</b>","gloss":"[za-VOOT] — no trick: т is already quiet","say":"зовут"}],"tasks":[{"type":"drag","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each word to the way it actually sounds.","q":"Match the spelling to the sound:","pairs":[["во́дка","[VOT-ka]"],["всё","[fsyo]"],["го́род","[GO-rat]"],["хле́б","[khlep]"]]},{"type":"speak","target":"всё","sub":"[fsyo] — start with a quiet f","want":"всё"}]}],"vocab":{"intro":"Today's words all hide the same trick — a loud twin going quiet. Flip each card, read the spelling with your eye, then say the lazy version with your mouth.","items":[{"en":"хле́б","ru":"bread","ex":"[khlep] — б quiets to p at the end"},{"en":"дру́г","ru":"friend","ex":"[drook] — г quiets to k"},{"en":"но́ж","ru":"knife","ex":"[nosh] — ж quiets to sh"},{"en":"го́род","ru":"city","ex":"[GO-rat] — reduction + devoicing in one word"},{"en":"всё","ru":"everything, all","ex":"[fsyo] — в quiets to f before с"},{"en":"во́дка","ru":"vodka","ex":"[VOT-ka] — д quiets to t before к"},{"en":"за́втрак","ru":"breakfast","ex":"[ZAF-trak] — в quiets to f before т"},{"en":"зову́т","ru":"they call (used to say your name)","ex":"[za-VOOT] — Menyá zovút… = my name is…"}]},"drag":{"intro":"The spelling game. Drag each written word to the way a Russian actually says it — listen for the quiet endings.","pairs":[["хле́б","[khlep]"],["дру́г","[drook]"],["но́ж","[nosh]"],["го́род","[GO-rat]"],["всё","[fsyo]"],["во́дка","[VOT-ka]"],["за́втрак","[ZAF-trak]"]]},"reading":{"intro":"Read each one aloud the way it really sounds — quiet endings and all. Tap 🔊 to check yourself. The last line is a real sentence, and you can read it.","title":"Чита́ем! (Let's read)","sentences":["хле́б","дру́г","но́ж","всё","го́род","во́дка","за́втрак","Меня́ зову́т А́нна."],"translation":"bread · friend · knife · everything · city · vodka · breakfast · My name is Anna."},"quiz":{"intro":"Last stretch — five quick checks and the lazy-endings trick is yours for good. 💛","items":[{"q":"1. Read it: <b>хлеб</b>. The final letter sounds like —","opts":["b","kh","p"],"answer":2,"explain":"At the end of a word the loud <b>б</b> switches its voice off: [khlep]. The spelling keeps the б — only the sound relaxes."},{"q":"2. Which two letters are voicing twins — same mouth, voice on/off?","opts":["М and Н","З and С","Л and Р"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>з/с</b> is the throat-test pair — same hiss, buzz on or off. М and Н use different mouth positions, and Л/Р aren't a pair at all."},{"q":"3. <b>друг</b> (friend) is pronounced —","opts":["[drook]","[drug]","[droozh]"],"answer":0,"explain":"The final <b>г</b> devoices to its quiet twin <b>к</b>: [drook]. The g sound survives when a vowel follows, as in дру́га (of a friend)."},{"q":"4. In <b>всё</b>, the first letter <b>в</b> sounds like —","opts":["v — as always","f — it quiets before the с","nothing — it's silent"],"answer":1,"explain":"A loud twin right before a quiet consonant goes quiet too: <b>вс</b> → [fs], so всё = [fsyo]."},{"q":"5. <b>го́род</b> = [GO-rat]. Which two rules teamed up there?","opts":["Soft consonants + the hard sign","Vowel reduction + final devoicing","The жи/ши rule + a silent letter"],"answer":1,"explain":"The unstressed <b>о</b> reduces to [a] (Lesson 9) and the final <b>д</b> quiets to [t] (today). Two rules, one word — that's real Russian reading."}]}},
"12": {"day":12,"week":"02","themeRu":"Read for real!","themeEn":"Чита́ем предложе́ния","intro":"This is it — the final lesson. Today you get the last few reading tricks Russians use without even thinking (including a sneaky letter that secretly sounds like [v]), and then we do the real thing: you read whole Russian sentences out loud. Twelve lessons ago you couldn't read a single letter. Watch what you do today. 💛","introAudio":"This is it, the final lesson. Today you get the last few reading tricks Russians use without even thinking, including a sneaky letter that secretly sounds like v. And then we do the real thing: you read whole Russian sentences out loud. Twelve lessons ago you couldn't read a single letter. Watch what you do today.","goals":["Spot the sneaky g that sounds like [v] in common endings and everyday words","Master three rapid-fire tricks: hard [zhy]/[shy], the [tsa] verb ending, and the two always-soft consonants","Learn how to read a whole sentence: stress first, word by word, then flow","Read all seven course sentences aloud — real Russian, in your voice"],"learned":["Caught <b>г</b> sounding like [v] in <b>-ого / -его</b> and in <b>его́</b>, <b>сего́дня</b>, <b>ничего́</b>","Nailed the rapid-fire tricks: <b>жи/ши</b> = [zhy/shy], <b>-тся/-ться</b> = [tsa], <b>ч/щ</b> always soft","Read a sentence the reader's way: stress first, chunk by chunk, then flow","Read seven real Russian sentences aloud — the whole course, out loud"],"review":{"intro":"Before the finale — one quick look back at Lesson 11. The loud twins (voiced consonants) go quiet at the end of a word. Do you still hear it?","tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"<b>хле́б</b> (bread) — the final <b>б</b> actually sounds like —","opts":["[b], exactly as written","[p] — it goes quiet at the end","[v]"],"answer":1,"explain":"Voiced <b>б</b> devoices at the end of a word: <b>хле́б</b> = [khlep]. You remembered."},{"type":"choice","q":"<b>дру́г</b> (friend) ends in which sound?","opts":["[k]","[g]","[kh]"],"answer":0,"explain":"Final <b>г</b> devoices to [k]: <b>дру́г</b> = [drook]. Same rule, different letter."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the sound you actually say, in English letters.","q":"<b>но́ж</b> (knife) — the voiced <b>ж</b> goes quiet at the end. Type the sound you really hear:","accept":["sh"],"placeholder":"in English letters…"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Tricks 1 · The sneaky г","title":"When <em>г</em> secretly sounds like [v]","paras":["One last spelling secret, and Russian stops hiding anything from you. In the endings <b>-ого</b> and <b>-его</b>, the letter <b>г</b> is pronounced <b>[v]</b> — not [g]. It's a very old habit of spoken Russian that the spelling never caught up with.","The same hidden [v] lives in a few everyday words: <b>его́</b> (his / him) = [yi-VO], <b>сего́дня</b> (today) = [si-VOD-nya], <b>ничего́</b> (nothing / no problem) = [ni-chi-VO]. This one trick unlocks a whole sentence you'll read today: <b>Сего́дня хоро́шая пого́да.</b>"],"audio":"One last spelling secret, and Russian stops hiding anything from you. In the endings ого and его, the letter г is pronounced v, not g. The same hidden v lives in a few everyday words: его, yi-VO. сегодня, si-VOD-nya. ничего, ni-chi-VO. This one trick unlocks a whole sentence you'll read today: Сегодня хорошая погода.","table":{"rows":[["его́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"его\">🔊</button>","[yi-VO] — his / him: the г is a [v]"],["сего́дня <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сегодня\">🔊</button>","[si-VOD-nya] — today"],["ничего́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ничего\">🔊</button>","[ni-chi-VO] — nothing / “no problem”"],["хоро́шего дня́! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"хорошего дня\">🔊</button>","[kha-RO-shy-va DNYA] — have a good day"]]},"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the word, then tap how it really sounds. Wrong taps are working moments — they're how it sticks.","q":"How does the ending <b>-ого</b> sound in <b>ничего́</b>?","opts":["[-oh-go], exactly as written","[-avo] — the г turns into [v]","[-okho]"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ничего́</b> = [ni-chi-VO]. In <b>-ого/-его</b> the <b>г</b> always turns into [v] — spelling keeps the old letter, speech moved on."},{"type":"choice","q":"You read <b>сего́дня</b> (today). Which sound does the <b>г</b> make here?","opts":["[g] — exactly as written","[v] — the -его trick in action","[k] — it devoices"],"answer":1,"explain":"In сего́дня the г hides a [v] — the same family as -ого/-его and его́: [si-VOD-nya]."}]},{"eyebrow":"Tricks 2 · Rapid-fire","title":"жи/ши, the vanishing <em>-тся</em>, and the always-soft pair","paras":["Three quick tricks, rapid-fire. First: <b>жи</b> and <b>ши</b>. Spelling writes <b>и</b> after ж and ш, but the sound stays hard — say [zhy] and [shy], as if it were written жы/шы. The и simply can't soften those two.","Second: the verb endings <b>-тся</b> and <b>-ться</b> both collapse into one quick <b>[tsa]</b> — <b>у́чится</b> (studies) = [OO-chi-tsa]. Third: <b>ч</b> and <b>щ</b> are the mirror image of ж/ш — they are <i>always</i> soft, and nothing after them can harden them. So <b>ча́й</b> is a gentle [chay], every time. That's it — you now hold every reading trick this course has to give."],"audio":"Three quick tricks, rapid-fire. First: жи and ши. Spelling writes и after ж and ш, but the sound stays hard: zhy, shy. Second: the verb endings тся and ться both collapse into one quick tsa. учится: OO-chi-tsa. Third: ч and щ are always soft, and nothing after them can harden them. So чай is a gentle chay, every time. That's it. You now hold every reading trick this course has to give.","examples":[{"ru":"lives","en":"<b>живёт</b>","gloss":"[zhy-VYOT] — hard [zhy]","say":"живёт"},{"ru":"car","en":"<b>маши́на</b>","gloss":"[ma-SHY-na] — hard [shy]","say":"машина"},{"ru":"he / she studies","en":"<b>у́чится</b>","gloss":"[OO-chi-tsa] — -тся = [tsa]","say":"учится"},{"ru":"tea","en":"<b>ча́й</b>","gloss":"[chay] — ч is always soft","say":"чай"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The <b>жи</b> in <b>живёт</b> (lives) sounds like —","opts":["[zhy] — hard, as if written жы","[zhee] — soft, exactly as written","[jee], as in English “jeep”"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>жи</b> always sounds hard: [zhy]. The и after ж is pure spelling tradition — your ear can ignore it."},{"type":"choice","q":"<b>у́чится</b> (he/she studies) — how do the final letters <b>-тся</b> sound?","opts":["[t-sya], two clear parts","[cha]","[tsa], one quick sound"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>-тся</b> and <b>-ться</b> both melt into a single [tsa]: <b>у́чится</b> = [OO-chi-tsa]. One sound, not two."}]},{"eyebrow":"Read for real · Whole sentences","title":"How to read a <em>whole sentence</em>","paras":["A sentence is just words in a row — and you already know how to read words. The method: <b>stress first</b>. Find the loud syllable of each word, say each word slowly on its own, then let them flow together as one line.","Let's walk one end-to-end: <b>Меня́ зову́т А́нна.</b> (My name is Anna.) Word one, <b>меня́</b> — stress at the end, so the unstressed <b>е</b> fades to [i]: [mi-NYA]. Word two, <b>зову́т</b> — the unstressed <b>о</b> fades to [a]: [za-VOOT]. Word three, <b>А́нна</b> — [AN-na]. Now glue them: [mi-NYA za-VOOT AN-na]. Slowly first, then smoothly — that's you, reading real Russian out loud."],"audio":"A sentence is just words in a row, and you already know how to read words. The method: stress first. Find the loud syllable of each word, say each word slowly on its own, then let them flow together. Let's walk one end-to-end: Меня зовут Анна. Word one, меня: mi-NYA. Word two, зовут: za-VOOT. Word three, Анна: AN-na. Now glue them: Меня зовут Анна. Slowly first, then smoothly. That's you, reading real Russian out loud.","examples":[{"ru":"My name is Anna.","en":"<b>Меня́ зову́т А́нна.</b>","gloss":"[mi-NYA za-VOOT AN-na]","say":"Меня зовут Анна."},{"ru":"I read in Russian.","en":"<b>Я чита́ю по-ру́сски.</b>","gloss":"[ya chi-TA-yu pa-ROO-ski]","say":"Я читаю по-русски."}],"tasks":[{"type":"speak","target":"Меня́ зову́т А́нна.","sub":"[mi-NYA za-VOOT AN-na] — stress the loud parts, let the rest fade","want":"Меня зовут Анна."},{"type":"speak","target":"Спаси́бо большо́е!","sub":"[spa-SEE-ba bal-SHO-ye] — thank you very much","want":"Спасибо большое!"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the meaning in English.","q":"You just read <b>Э́то мой до́м.</b> Write what it means in English:","accept":["this is my house","this is my home","it is my house","it's my house","it is my home","it's my home","that is my house","that’s my house","thats my house","that is my home","that’s my home","thats my home"],"placeholder":"in English…"}]}],"vocab":{"intro":"The words that carry today's sentences. Flip each card — and notice how many tricks you can now spot hiding inside them.","items":[{"en":"сего́дня","ru":"today","ex":"[si-VOD-nya] — the г is a secret [v]"},{"en":"ничего́","ru":"nothing; “no problem”","ex":"[ni-chi-VO] — another secret [v]"},{"en":"пого́да","ru":"weather","ex":"[pa-GO-da] — unstressed о fades to [a]"},{"en":"хоро́шая","ru":"good, nice","ex":"[kha-RO-sha-ya] — nice (weather, day…)"},{"en":"большо́е","ru":"big; (here) “very much”","ex":"[bal-SHO-ye] — Спаси́бо большо́е!"},{"en":"извини́те","ru":"excuse me","ex":"[iz-vi-NEE-tye] — the polite opener"},{"en":"метро́","ru":"metro, subway","ex":"[mi-TRO] — stress on the end"},{"en":"чита́ю","ru":"I read","ex":"[chi-TA-yu] — Я чита́ю по-ру́сски."}]},"drag":{"intro":"One last matching game. Drag each spelling to the way it actually sounds.","pairs":[["его́","yi-VO — the г turns into [v]"],["сего́дня","si-VOD-nya — today"],["ничего́","ni-chi-VO — nothing"],["жи / ши","zhy / shy — hard, despite the и"],["-тся / -ться","tsa — one quick sound"],["ча́й","chay — ч is always soft"]]},"reading":{"intro":"Here it is — the moment the whole course was building toward. Read each sentence aloud, slowly, stress first, then let it flow. Tap 🔊 to compare. Every one of these is real Russian, and you can read it.","title":"Чита́ем предложе́ния! (Let's read sentences)","sentences":["Меня́ зову́т А́нна.","Как тебя́ зову́т?","Э́то мой до́м.","Я чита́ю по-ру́сски.","Сего́дня хоро́шая пого́да.","Спаси́бо большо́е!","Извини́те, где метро́?"],"translation":"My name is Anna. · What's your name? · This is my house. · I read in Russian. · The weather is nice today. · Thank you very much! · Excuse me, where is the metro?"},"quiz":{"intro":"The final check of the whole course. Twelve lessons ago, Cyrillic was a wall of strange shapes — today you read real Russian sentences out loud, and that is exactly what reading means. Take your time here. And when you're ready for words to start carrying meaning, grammar and conversation, Russian A1 Day 1 is waiting — you'll walk in already reading. 💛","items":[{"q":"1. Read it: <b>ничего́</b> (nothing / “no problem”). The <b>г</b> sounds like —","opts":["[g], as always","[kh]","[v] — ничего́ = [ni-chi-VO]"],"answer":2,"explain":"In <b>-ого/-его</b> and in <b>его́ / сего́дня / ничего́</b>, the <b>г</b> is pronounced [v]. The last secret — and now it's yours."},{"q":"2. Which pair of consonants is <i>always</i> soft, no matter what follows?","opts":["ч and щ","ж and ш","т and с"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>ч</b> and <b>щ</b> are always soft; <b>ж</b> and <b>ш</b> are the opposite — always hard. <b>т</b> and <b>с</b> can go either way."},{"q":"3. The <b>ши</b> in <b>маши́на</b> (car) is pronounced —","opts":["[shee], soft — exactly as written","[shy], hard — as if written шы","[see]"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ж</b> and <b>ш</b> stay hard whatever follows: <b>маши́на</b> = [ma-SHY-na]. The и is spelling tradition, nothing more."},{"q":"4. You read: <b>Извини́те, где метро́?</b> You would say this to —","opts":["order a coffee","say goodbye to a friend","politely ask where the metro is"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>Извини́те</b> = excuse me, <b>где</b> = where, <b>метро́</b> = the metro. You didn't just sound it out — you understood it."},{"q":"5. <b>Сего́дня хоро́шая пого́да.</b> means —","opts":["The metro is closed today.","The weather is nice today.","My house is very big."],"answer":1,"explain":"[si-VOD-nya kha-RO-sha-ya pa-GO-da] — <b>сего́дня</b> = today, <b>хоро́шая</b> = nice, <b>пого́да</b> = weather. A sentence with a hidden [v] and vowel reduction — and you read it anyway."},{"q":"6. Last question of the course. At Lesson 1 you could read zero Russian letters. What can you read now?","opts":["Whole real Russian sentences, out loud","Only the five letters from Lesson 1","Nothing has really changed"],"answer":0,"explain":"All 33 letters, stress, reduction, soft and hard consonants, devoicing, and today's final tricks — you read <b>real Russian sentences</b> now. That's a genuine skill, and it's yours for good. Whenever you feel like going further, Russian A1 Day 1 picks up right here. Спаси́бо большо́е — and welcome to reading Russian. 💛"}]}}
};
